# Master Textbook Structured Repository for all 38 Chapters
MASTER_TEXTBOOK_DATA = {('EM', 0): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Discrete Mathematics - Mathematical Logic & Set Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Strict Courtroom Contract Clause', 'text': "• Proposition: Like entering a Wi-Fi password—it is strictly True or False. Sentences like 'What time is it?' are not propositions.\n• Implication (P -> Q): Dad promises 'If you score AIR 1 (P), I will buy you a bike (Q)'. He only breaks his word if you get AIR 1 and he refuses to buy the bike (T -> F = False). If you do not get AIR 1, whatever happens never violates the promise (Vacuous Truth)!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '1.1', 'title': 'Propositions & The 5 Core Logic Connectives (Complete Truth Table)', 'content': "• <b>What is a Proposition?</b> A declarative sentence that is either strictly True (T) or strictly False (F), but never both.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- '5 is a prime number' is a proposition (True).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 'Please close the door' is NOT a proposition (command).<br/><br/>• <b>Complete Master Truth Table for the 5 Operators:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>P   Q   ~P (NOT)   P ∧ Q (AND)   P ∨ Q (OR)   P → Q (Implication)   P ↔ Q (Biconditional)<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>T   T      F           T             T                 T                       T<br/>T   F      F           F             T                 F (Broken promise!)     F<br/>F   T      T           F             T                 T (Vacuously true)      F<br/>F   F      T           F             F                 T (Vacuously true)      T</div>• <b>Crucial Implication Identity:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>(P → Q) ≡ (~P ∨ Q)</code> (Use this identity to rewrite implications in 99% of GATE proofs!).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: P → Q is False ONLY when P = True and Q = False! When P is False, (P → Q) is 100% GUARANTEED to be True regardless of Q (Vacuous Truth).'}, {'number': '1.2', 'title': 'Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse', 'content': "• For any conditional statement <b>P → Q</b> ('If P, then Q'):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Contrapositive (~Q → ~P):</b> 'If NOT Q, then NOT P'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>100% Logically Equivalent:</b> <code>(P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Proving the contrapositive proves the original statement!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Converse (Q → P):</b> 'If Q, then P'. NOT logically equivalent to P → Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Inverse (~P → ~Q):</b> 'If NOT P, then NOT Q'. Equivalent to the Converse, NOT the original.<br/><br/>• <b>Example:</b> 'If it rains (P), the grass is wet (Q)':<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Contrapositive: 'If the grass is NOT wet (~Q), it did NOT rain (~P)' (True!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Converse: 'If grass is wet, it rained' (False! A sprinkler could have caused it).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Converse (Q → P) and Inverse (~P → ~Q) are logically equivalent to EACH OTHER, but NEVER to the original statement!'}, {'number': '1.3', 'title': "First-Order Logic: Quantifiers (∀ vs ∃) & De Morgan's Rules", 'content': "• <b>Universal Quantifier (∀x):</b> 'For all x'. True only if P(x) holds for EVERY single element in the universe.<br/>• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃x):</b> 'There exists an x'. True if at least ONE element makes P(x) true.<br/><br/>• <b>De Morgan's Negation Rules for Quantifiers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <code>~[ ∀x P(x) ] ≡ ∃x [ ~P(x) ]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Negation of 'All students passed' is 'There is at least one student who did NOT pass'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <code>~[ ∃x P(x) ] ≡ ∀x [ ~P(x) ]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Negation of 'Someone scored 100' is 'Everyone scored not 100'.<br/><br/>• <b>Order of Quantifiers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>∀x ∃y P(x, y)</code> (Everyone loves someone) is NOT equal to <code>∃y ∀x P(x, y)</code> (There is one specific person whom everybody loves!).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Negating 'All X are Y' is 'There exists an X that is NOT Y'! It is NEVER 'No X are Y'. Negating ∀ gives ∃, and negating ∃ gives ∀."}, {'number': '1.4', 'title': 'Relations & Properties (Counting Formulas for Set of Size n)', 'content': "• Let set A contain <b>n</b> elements. The Cartesian product A × A contains <b>n²</b> ordered pairs.<br/>• <b>The 5 Fundamental Counting Formulas to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>1. Total Relations:               2^(n²)<br/>2. Reflexive Relations:           2^(n² - n)<br/>3. Symmetric Relations:           2^(n(n + 1) / 2)<br/>4. Reflexive & Symmetric:         2^(n(n - 1) / 2)<br/>5. Equivalence Relations:         Bell Numbers B(n): B(1)=1, B(2)=2, B(3)=5, B(4)=15, B(5)=52</div>• <b>Equivalence Relation Definition:</b> A relation that is simultaneously <b>Reflexive</b>, <b>Symmetric</b>, and <b>Transitive</b>. It partitions the set into disjoint Equivalence Classes.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a set with 4 elements, total equivalence relations = Bell Number B(4) = 15! Reflexive relations = 2^(16 - 4) = 2^12 = 4096.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Counting Symmetric Relations', 'problem': 'Set A has 4 elements: A = {1, 2, 3, 4}. How many symmetric relations can be formed on set A?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for symmetric relations on a set of size n is: Total = 2^(n(n+1)/2).', 'Step 2: Here n = 4.', 'Step 3: Exponent = (4 × (4 + 1)) / 2 = (4 × 5) / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10.', 'Step 4: Compute 2^10 = 1024.'], 'answer': 'Total Symmetric Relations = 1024.'}], 'exam_traps': ['A relation can be BOTH symmetric and anti-symmetric at the same time (e.g. Identity relation on any set)!', 'Empty relation on a non-empty set is NOT reflexive, but it is vacuously symmetric and transitive!']}, ('EM', 1): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Combinatorics, Recurrence Relations & Graph Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Sock Drawer in the Dark & Party Handshakes', 'text': '• Pigeonhole Principle: If a drawer has black and brown socks, pulling 3 socks guarantees at least one matching pair because 3 socks go into 2 color buckets.\n• Handshaking Theorem: At any party, every handshake involves 2 people. So if you sum all individual handshakes counted by each person, the sum is ALWAYS an even number!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '2.1', 'title': 'Pigeonhole Principle (PHP) & Guaranteed Matches', 'content': '• <b>Basic Pigeonhole Principle:</b> If <code>k + 1</code> or more objects are placed into <code>k</code> boxes, then at least one box must contain 2 or more objects.<br/>• <b>Generalized PHP:</b> If <code>N</code> objects are placed into <code>k</code> boxes, at least one box contains at least <b>⌈N / k⌉</b> objects.<br/>• <b>Inverse PHP Question (Common in GATE):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;To guarantee that at least <b>m</b> items land in the same box out of <b>k</b> boxes:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Minimum Items Needed = k · (m - 1) + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Example:</i> To guarantee 5 cards of the same suit (4 suits): 4 · (5 - 1) + 1 = 17 cards.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In guaranteed match questions: Worst-case scenario is having (m - 1) items in every single box. Adding 1 more item triggers the guarantee: N_min = k(m - 1) + 1!'}, {'number': '2.2', 'title': 'Graph Theory: Handshaking Theorem & Planar Graphs', 'content': "• <b>Handshaking Lemma:</b> In any undirected graph: <b>∑ deg(v) = 2|E|</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Corollary:</i> In every graph, the number of vertices with ODD degree is ALWAYS EVEN!<br/>• <b>Complete Graph (K_n):</b> Total edges = <b>n(n - 1) / 2</b>.<br/>• <b>Bipartite Graph:</b> Graph is bipartite IF AND ONLY IF it has <b>NO odd-length cycles</b> (2-colorable).<br/><br/>• <b>Planar Graphs & Euler's Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>V - E + R = 2</b> (where R is total regions/faces including the infinite exterior face).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Maximum edges in any simple connected planar graph: <b>E ≤ 3V - 6</b> (for V ≥ 3).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If triangle-free (no C_3 cycle): <b>E ≤ 2V - 4</b>.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: K_5 (complete graph on 5 vertices) and K_{3,3} (complete bipartite utility graph) are the two minimal non-planar graphs (Kuratowski's Theorem)!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Planar Graph Faces Calculation', 'problem': 'A connected planar graph has 8 vertices and each vertex has degree 3. How many faces (regions) does it divide the plane into?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate total edges using Handshaking Theorem: ∑ deg(v) = 2E.', 'Step 2: 8 vertices with degree 3 ⇒ Total degree sum = 8 × 3 = 24.', 'Step 3: 2E = 24 ⇒ E = 12 edges.', "Step 4: Use Euler's planar formula: V - E + R = 2.", 'Step 5: Substitute V = 8, E = 12: 8 - 12 + R = 2 ⇒ -4 + R = 2 ⇒ R = 6.'], 'answer': 'Total regions R = 6 (5 bounded faces + 1 unbounded outer face).'}], 'exam_traps': ["Euler's formula V - E + R = 2 requires the graph to be CONNECTED. If disconnected with k components: V - E + R = 1 + k.", 'Sum of degrees in any graph is ALWAYS an even number. If an exam question presents degree sequence with an odd sum, such a graph CANNOT exist!']}, ('EM', 2): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Linear Algebra - Matrices, Systems of Equations & Eigenvalues', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: 3D Video Game Graphics & Bridge Resonance', 'text': '• Matrices: In 3D games, moving and rotating a character is simply multiplying 3D coordinate vectors by 4x4 transformation matrices.\n• Eigenvalues: The natural resonance frequencies of a bridge or skyscraper. If earthquake vibrations match an eigenvalue frequency, catastrophic collapse happens!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '3.1', 'title': 'System of Linear Equations (AX = B) Consistency', 'content': '• Consider system <b>AX = B</b> with n variables. Let [A | B] be the augmented matrix.<br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Consistency Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Inconsistent (No Solution):</b> <code>Rank(A) ≠ Rank(A | B)</code> (i.e. Rank(A) < Rank(A | B)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Unique Solution:</b> <code>Rank(A) == Rank(A | B) == n</code> (Full rank equal to number of unknowns).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Infinitely Many Solutions:</b> <code>Rank(A) == Rank(A | B) = r < n</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of linearly independent free variables = <b>n - r</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Homogeneous System (AX = 0):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Always consistent (trivial solution X = 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Has non-zero (non-trivial) solutions IF AND ONLY IF <b>det(A) = 0</b> (Rank(A) < n).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Homogeneous system AX = 0 has non-trivial solutions IF AND ONLY IF det(A) = 0. For non-homogeneous AX = B, check Rank(A) vs Rank(A|B)!'}, {'number': '3.2', 'title': 'Eigenvalues & Eigenvectors: The 5 Golden Properties', 'content': '• Characteristic Equation: <code>det(A - λI) = 0</code>.<br/>• <b>The 5 Golden Properties for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sum of Eigenvalues = Trace of A</b> (sum of main diagonal elements): <code>∑ λ_i = Tr(A)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Product of Eigenvalues = Determinant of A</b>: <code>∏ λ_i = det(A)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Triangular / Diagonal Matrices:</b> Eigenvalues are SIMPLY the main diagonal entries!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. If λ is an eigenvalue of A:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^k has eigenvalue <b>λ^k</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^(-1) has eigenvalue <b>1 / λ</b> (provided λ ≠ 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- (A + cI) has eigenvalue <b>λ + c</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Real Symmetric Matrices:</b> Always have strictly REAL eigenvalues.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 90% of GATE eigenvalue questions, DO NOT solve the cubic equation! Just verify Trace = Sum(λ) and Det = Product(λ) against the 4 options to find the answer in 10 seconds!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Finding Eigenvalues using Trace and Det', 'problem': 'Find the eigenvalues of A = [[3, 2], [1, 4]].', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate Trace(A) = sum of main diagonal = 3 + 4 = 7.', 'Step 2: Calculate Det(A) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 1) = 12 - 2 = 10.', 'Step 3: Sum of eigenvalues λ1 + λ2 = 7 and Product λ1 · λ2 = 10.', 'Step 4: Two numbers that add to 7 and multiply to 10 are 2 and 5.'], 'answer': 'Eigenvalues are λ1 = 2, λ2 = 5.'}], 'exam_traps': ['det(A + B) is NOT det(A) + det(B)! However, det(A × B) = det(A) × det(B).', 'Scaling an n × n matrix by constant k scales determinant by k^n: det(k·A) = k^n · det(A)!']}, ('EM', 3): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': "Calculus, Probability Distributions & Bayes' Theorem", 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Analogy: The Rare Disease Medical Test (Bayes' Paradox)", 'text': "• Bayes' Theorem: A medical test is 99% accurate, but a disease affects only 1 in 10,000 people. If you test positive, what is the chance you actually have the disease? Surprisingly, only ~1%! Because false positives from the 9,999 healthy people vastly outnumber true positives from the 1 sick person."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '4.1', 'title': "Bayes' Theorem & Conditional Probability", 'content': "• <b>Conditional Probability Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Independent Events:</b> Events A and B are independent if and only if <b>P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Bayes' Rule Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A_i | B) = [ P(B | A_i) · P(A_i) ] / ∑ [ P(B | A_j) · P(A_j) ]</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Where the denominator is the Law of Total Probability P(B).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Mutually exclusive means they cannot occur together (P(A ∩ B) = 0). Independent means occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. They are completely different concepts!'}, {'number': '4.2', 'title': 'Probability Distributions: Binomial, Poisson & Normal', 'content': '• <b>1. Binomial Distribution B(n, p):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- P(X = k) = C(n, k) · p^k · (1 - p)^{n - k}.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>n · p</b>; Variance = <b>n · p · (1 - p)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>2. Poisson Distribution (Rate λ):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Models rare events: P(X = k) = (e^(-λ) · λ^k) / k!.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Unique Property:</b> Mean = <b>λ</b> and Variance = <b>λ</b> (Mean == Variance!).<br/><br/>• <b>3. Uniform Distribution on [a, b]:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>(a + b) / 2</b>; Variance = <b>(b - a)² / 12</b>.', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Poisson distribution, Mean = Variance = λ! If a question states 'the variance of a Poisson variable is 9', then immediately mean = 9 and λ = 9!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Bayes' Theorem in Factory Production", 'problem': 'Factory A produces 70% of items with 1% defect rate. Factory B produces 30% of items with 5% defect rate. An item chosen at random is defective. What is the probability it was made by Factory B?', 'steps': ['Step 1: P(A) = 0.70, P(B) = 0.30.', 'Step 2: P(D | A) = 0.01, P(D | B) = 0.05.', 'Step 3: Total Probability P(D) = (0.70 × 0.01) + (0.30 × 0.05) = 0.007 + 0.015 = 0.022.', "Step 4: By Bayes' Theorem: P(B | D) = [ P(B) · P(D | B) ] / P(D) = 0.015 / 0.022 = 15 / 22 ≈ 0.6818."], 'answer': 'P(Factory B | Defective) = 15 / 22 (approx 68.18%).'}], 'exam_traps': ['Variance can NEVER be negative! Var(X) = E[X²] - (E[X])² ≥ 0.', 'Scaling rule: Var(aX + b) = a² · Var(X). The constant b adds 0 to variance!']}, ('DL', 0): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Number Systems, Signed Numbers & IEEE-754 Floating Point', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Odometer Rollback & Game High Score Overflow', 'text': "• 2's Complement: Like a car odometer rolling backward from 00000 to 99999. In an 8-bit binary computer, rolling backward from 00000000 gives 11111111, which represents -1!\n• Overflow: In early arcade games (like Pac-Man level 256), adding 1 to 255 caused the 8-bit byte to overflow back to 0, breaking the game."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '5.1', 'title': "Signed Number Ranges & 2's Complement Arithmetic", 'content': "• <b>For an n-bit binary representation:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Representation    Range for n bits                Number of Zeros   Special Notes<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Unsigned          0 to 2^n - 1                    1 (00...0)        All bits represent magnitude<br/>Sign-Magnitude    -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1) 2 (+0 and -0)    MSB is sign bit<br/>1's Complement    -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1) 2 (+0 and -0)    Bitwise inversion<br/>2's Complement    -2^(n-1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)      1 (Unique zero!)  Standard in all modern CPUs</div>• <b>Asymmetry in 2's Complement:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For n = 8 bits: Range is <b>-128 to +127</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Notice that <b>-128 has NO positive counterpart</b> in 8 bits! Attempting to negate -128 causes integer overflow.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 2's complement with n bits: The minimum negative number is -2^(n-1). It has no positive counterpart! Fast 2's complement shortcut: Copy bits from right to left up to and including the first '1', then flip all remaining bits to the left!"}, {'number': '5.2', 'title': 'IEEE-754 Single Precision 32-bit Floating Point', 'content': "• <b>Bit Fields: [ Sign (1 bit) | Biased Exponent E (8 bits) | Mantissa M (23 bits) ]</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Field          Bit Count   Function<br/>--------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Sign (S)       1 bit       0 for Positive, 1 for Negative<br/>Exponent (E)   8 bits      Excess-127 Bias: Stored E = actual exponent e + 127<br/>Mantissa (M)   23 bits     Fractional part with implicit leading 1: 1.M</div>• <b>Value Formula:</b> <code>(-1)^S × (1.M) × 2^(E - 127)</code>.<br/>• <b>Special Patterns:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 255 and M = 0: Represents <b>±Infinity</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 255 and M ≠ 0: Represents <b>NaN (Not a Number)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 0 and M = 0: Represents <b>±0</b>.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The leading '1' in normalized floating point (1.M) is NEVER stored in memory—it is implicit! This gives 24 bits of precision from 23 stored mantissa bits."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: IEEE-754 Representation of -13.625', 'problem': 'Convert -13.625 into IEEE-754 32-bit hexadecimal format.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Sign is negative ⇒ S = 1.', 'Step 2: 13.625 in binary: 13 = 1101; 0.625 = 0.101 ⇒ 1101.101.', 'Step 3: Normalize: 1101.101 = 1.101101 × 2^3.', 'Step 4: Actual exponent e = 3 ⇒ Stored E = 3 + 127 = 130 = 10000010 in binary.', 'Step 5: Mantissa M = 101101 followed by 17 zeros.', 'Step 6: Combine: 1 | 10000010 | 10110100000000000000000. In Hex: 0xC15A0000.'], 'answer': 'Hexadecimal value = 0xC15A0000.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In IEEE-754, the smallest positive normalized number is 1.0 × 2^(-126) (where E = 1, not E = 0!).', 'Double precision IEEE-754 uses 64 bits: 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits (bias = 1023), and 52 mantissa bits.']}, ('DL', 1): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Boolean Algebra, Logic Gates & K-Map Minimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Universal LEGO Brick & Map Minimizer', 'text': '• Universal Gates: Just as LEGO bricks can build any spaceship, NAND and NOR gates alone can build any computer circuit.\n• K-Map: A 2D puzzle where adjacent boxes differ by only 1 bit. Grouping 1s cancels out changing variables, shrinking circuit size.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '6.1', 'title': 'Boolean Laws & The Consensus Theorem', 'content': "• <b>Consensus Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AB + A'C + BC = AB + A'C</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Variable A appears in true form and complemented form (A, A').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The term <b>BC</b> is redundant (consensus) and can be completely deleted!<br/><br/>• <b>Minimum Gate Counts for Universal Gates:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Target Gate   NAND Gates Required   NOR Gates Required<br/>-------------------------------------------------------<br/>NOT                  1                     1<br/>AND                  2                     3<br/>OR                   3                     2<br/>XOR                  4                     5<br/>XNOR                 5                     4</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: XOR requires 4 NAND gates (or 5 NOR gates). XNOR requires 5 NAND gates (or 4 NOR gates). Notice the perfect reciprocal symmetry: 4 vs 5!'}, {'number': '6.2', 'title': 'Karnaugh Maps (K-Maps), Prime & Essential Prime Implicants', 'content': "• <b>Implicant:</b> Any product term that covers one or more 1s.<br/>• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> A rectangular group of size 2^k (1, 2, 4, 8) that CANNOT be merged into a larger group.<br/>• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A Prime Implicant that covers at least one '1' that is NOT covered by any other prime implicant.<br/>• <b>Minimal SOP Formula:</b> MUST include ALL Essential Prime Implicants!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every EPI is a PI, but not every PI is an EPI! To find EPIs fast: look for a '1' that belongs to only ONE valid largest rectangle."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Boolean Function Minimization', 'problem': "Simplify F = AB + A'C + BC using Boolean laws.", 'steps': ["Step 1: Check terms: AB, A'C, BC.", "Step 2: Variable A appears complemented (A and A').", 'Step 3: The third term BC is the product of remaining variables.', 'Step 4: By Consensus Theorem, BC is redundant and can be deleted.'], 'answer': "Simplified function: F = AB + A'C."}], 'exam_traps': ["In K-maps, grouping 0s gives the minimized POS expression (F'), not SOP!", "Don't care terms (X) can be included to enlarge groups, but you are NEVER required to cover all don't cares."]}, ('DL', 2): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Combinational Circuits - Multiplexers, Decoders & Adders', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Train Track Switch & Letter Mail Sorter', 'text': '• Multiplexer (MUX): Like a railway switch choosing which incoming track connects to the single outgoing main line, controlled by switch levers (select lines).\n• Decoder: A mail sorter reading a zip code and directing the parcel to exactly one specific delivery box.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '7.1', 'title': 'Multiplexer (MUX) as a Universal Logic Module', 'content': "• A <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b> has 2^n data inputs, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/>• <b>Function Implementation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Using a <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b>: Connect all n variables directly to select lines. Connect truth table outputs (0 or 1) to data inputs. No extra gates needed!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Using a <b>2^(n-1) : 1 MUX</b>: Connect (n - 1) variables to select lines. The last variable X can provide data inputs as <b>0, 1, X, or X'</b>. May need an inverter for X'.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any Boolean function of n variables can be implemented using a single 2^(n-1) : 1 MUX with at most one external NOT gate!'}, {'number': '7.2', 'title': 'Half Adder vs Full Adder Architecture', 'content': '• <b>Half Adder (HA):</b> Adds 2 bits A and B.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <code>A ⊕ B</code>; Carry = <code>A · B</code>.<br/>• <b>Full Adder (FA):</b> Adds 3 bits: A, B, and Carry-in C_in.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <code>A ⊕ B ⊕ C_in</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry-out = <code>AB + C_in(A ⊕ B)</code>.<br/>• <b>Hardware Construction:</b> 1 Full Adder = <b>2 Half Adders + 1 OR gate</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To build an n-bit Ripple Carry Adder, you need 1 Half Adder and (n - 1) Full Adders. Total delay = n × (carry propagation delay).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Implementing Logic with 4:1 MUX', 'problem': 'Implement F(A, B, C) = ∑m(1, 2, 4, 7) using a 4:1 MUX with select lines S1 = A, S0 = B.', 'steps': ['Step 1: For AB = 00: m0(C=0)->0, m1(C=1)->1 ⇒ I0 = C.', "Step 2: For AB = 01: m2(C=0)->1, m3(C=1)->0 ⇒ I1 = C'.", "Step 3: For AB = 10: m4(C=0)->1, m5(C=1)->0 ⇒ I2 = C'.", 'Step 4: For AB = 11: m6(C=0)->0, m7(C=1)->1 ⇒ I3 = C.'], 'answer': "Inputs to 4:1 MUX: I0 = C, I1 = C', I2 = C', I3 = C."}], 'exam_traps': ['Pay strict attention to MSB vs LSB on select lines (S1 = A, S0 = B vs S1 = B, S0 = A)!', 'Active-low enables in decoders require invert logic before cascading.']}, ('DL', 3): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Sequential Circuits, Flip-Flops & Counters', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: High-Speed Camera Snapshot & Subway Turnstile', 'text': "• Flip-Flop: A high-speed camera taking a snapshot strictly on the rising edge of the photographer's flash. Inputs must stay stable before (setup time) and after (hold time) the flash.\n• Counter: A subway turnstile clicker that increments each time a passenger passes through, cycling back to zero when reaching its maximum count."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '8.1', 'title': 'Flip-Flop Characteristic Equations', 'content': "• <b>JK Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = J·Q' + K'·Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=0: Hold (Q)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=1: Reset (0)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=0: Set (1)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=1: Toggle (Q') - eliminates SR invalid state!<br/>• <b>D Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = D</code> (data storage).<br/>• <b>T Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q</code> (T=0: Hold, T=1: Toggle).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The characteristic equations must be on your fingertips: JK: Q_{next} = J·Q' + K'·Q | D: Q_{next} = D | T: Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q."}, {'number': '8.2', 'title': 'Setup Time, Hold Time & Max Clock Frequency', 'content': '• <b>Setup Time (t_setup):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable BEFORE active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Hold Time (t_hold):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable AFTER active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Minimum Clock Period (T_clk):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>T_clk ≥ t_{cq} + t_{comb} + t_{setup}</b>.<br/>• <b>Maximum Clock Frequency:</b> <code>f_max = 1 / T_clk_min</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To avoid Hold time violations: t_{cq_min} + t_{comb_min} ≥ t_{hold}. Notice that hold time is independent of clock frequency!'}, {'number': '8.3', 'title': 'Shift Register Counters: Ring vs Johnson', 'content': '• <b>For an n-flip-flop register:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Binary Counter:</b> Has <b>2^n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Ring Counter:</b> Output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Johnson (Twisted Ring) Counter:</b> Inverted output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>2n</b> unique states!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An n-flip-flop Johnson counter has 2n states, while an n-flip-flop Ring counter has only n states! Unused states: Ring has (2^n - n) unused states; Johnson has (2^n - 2n) unused states.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Maximum Clock Frequency', 'problem': 'A sequential circuit has flip-flop t_cq = 2 ns, t_setup = 1.5 ns, and combinational logic delay = 4.5 ns. What is the maximum clock frequency f_max?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for minimum clock period: T_clk ≥ t_cq + t_comb + t_setup.', 'Step 2: T_clk_min = 2 ns + 4.5 ns + 1.5 ns = 8 ns.', 'Step 3: Maximum frequency f_max = 1 / T_clk_min = 1 / (8 × 10^(-9) s) = 125 MHz.'], 'answer': 'Maximum clock frequency = 125 MHz.'}], 'exam_traps': ["In negative edge-triggered ripple counter: connecting Q to clock gives an UP counter; connecting Q' gives a DOWN counter!", 'Race-around condition in JK flip-flop occurs when J = 1, K = 1 and clock pulse width t_p > propagation delay t_pd in level-triggered latches.']}, ('COA', 0): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Machine Instructions & Addressing Modes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Four Ways to Find Treasure', 'text': "• Immediate: Having $50 cash right inside your wallet (operand is inside the instruction itself).\n• Direct: Looking at an address slip that says 'Go to Locker 405' (address field points straight to data in RAM).\n• Indirect: Looking at a slip that says 'Go to Desk 12 to find the key for Locker 405' (a pointer to a pointer).\n• Indexed / Base: Your room is 'Building B, Room 14' (Base Address of Building B + Room Offset 14)."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '9.1', 'title': 'Effective Address (EA) & Memory References in Addressing Modes', 'content': "• <b>What is Effective Address (EA)?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The actual physical memory address where the operand's data is stored in RAM.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Different addressing modes calculate EA in different ways to give programmers flexibility and speed.<br/><br/>• <b>Summary Table of Addressing Modes & Memory References:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Mode              Effective Address (EA)    Operand Location   Memory Accesses (Fetch + Execute)<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Immediate         No EA (part of opcode)    Inside Instruction 1 access (Fetch only)<br/>Direct (Absolute) EA = Address Field        M[Address Field]   2 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Read)<br/>Indirect          EA = M[Address Field]     M[M[Address]]      3 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Ptr + 1 Read)<br/>Register          No EA (CPU Register)      Register R         1 access (Fetch only - very fast!)<br/>Register Indirect EA = [Register R]         M[[Register R]]    2 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Read)<br/>PC-Relative       EA = PC + Offset          M[PC + Offset]     Used in Jumps/Branches<br/>Indexed / Base    EA = Base Reg + Index Reg M[Base + Index]    Used in Array Traversals</div>• <b>Step-by-Step Example of Indirect Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Suppose instruction <code>LOAD @500</code> is fetched from memory address 1000:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <i>Step 1 (Instruction Fetch):</i> CPU accesses address 1000 to read <code>LOAD @500</code> (1st memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <i>Step 2 (Pointer Fetch):</i> CPU reads memory at address 500. Memory location 500 contains the number 800 (2nd memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <i>Step 3 (Operand Fetch):</i> CPU reads memory at address 800 to get the actual data value 42 (3rd memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total memory accesses = 3.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In PC-Relative mode, PC is ALWAYS incremented to point to the next instruction BEFORE adding the offset: EA = (PC + 1 or PC + 2) + Offset. Watch out for word-length vs byte-length PC increments!'}, {'number': '9.2', 'title': 'Instruction Word Formats & Expanding Opcode Technique', 'content': '• <b>Why Expanding Opcodes?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- An instruction has a fixed bit width (e.g. 16 bits).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If some instructions need 3 register addresses and others need only 1, we can shorten or expand the opcode bits so no bits are wasted.<br/><br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Bit Budget Calculation:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Consider a 16-bit instruction format with 16 CPU registers (requires 4 bits per register since 2^4 = 16):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>3-Register Instruction:</b> [ Opcode (4 bits) | Reg1 (4) | Reg2 (4) | Reg3 (4) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Total 4-bit opcode patterns = 2^4 = 16. If we allocate 14 patterns for 3-register instructions, <b>2 patterns remain free</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>2-Register Instruction:</b> [ Opcode (8 bits) | Reg1 (4) | Reg2 (4) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The 2 unused patterns expand across the 4 extra bits: <b>2 × 2^4 = 32 instructions possible</b>!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Expanding Opcode Formula: If k patterns are left unused at the higher level, they multiply by 2^(extra bits freed) at the next level: New Instructions = (Unused Patterns) × 2^(Freed Bits) - Allocated.'}, {'number': '9.3', 'title': 'RISC vs CISC Architecture Differences', 'content': '• <b>RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Simple, uniform 1-cycle instructions.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strict Load/Store Architecture:</b> Arithmetic operations (ADD, SUB) can ONLY operate on CPU registers, NEVER directly on RAM!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Hardwired control unit (faster), large register file, heavily pipelined.<br/>• <b>CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Variable length, multi-cycle complex instructions (e.g. <code>ADD [1000], [2000]</code> directly adds memory locations).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Microprogrammed control unit with fewer general-purpose registers.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In RISC, ALU instructions CANNOT access memory! To add a memory variable to a register, you must do: LOAD R1, [Memory] -> ADD R2, R2, R1.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating Memory References in Addressing Modes', 'problem': 'An instruction stored at memory address 2000 has opcode and address field 500. Memory at 500 contains 900, and memory at 900 contains operand 75. How many total memory cycles are executed to fetch and run this instruction in Indirect Addressing Mode?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Fetch instruction from PC address 2000: 1 memory read cycle.', 'Step 2: Decode instruction: mode is Indirect. Read address field 500 to get pointer: 1 memory read cycle (returns 900).', 'Step 3: Read effective address 900 to get operand 75: 1 memory read cycle.', 'Step 4: Total memory references = 1 (fetch) + 1 (pointer fetch) + 1 (operand read) = 3 cycles.'], 'answer': 'Total memory references = 3.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Do not forget to count the 1st memory reference needed to fetch the instruction itself!', 'Immediate addressing requires 1 memory reference for instruction fetch, but 0 references for operand fetch.']}, ('COA', 1): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'ALU, Control Unit & Memory Interfacing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Shorthand Math Trick & Direct Highway Delivery', 'text': "• Booth's Algorithm: Like calculating 99 × 6 by doing (100 - 1) × 6 = 600 - 6 = 594. Instead of adding 99 six times, you subtract once and add once!\n• DMA: Like a shipping company delivering pallets directly to the store warehouse without waiting for the CEO to personally inspect every single carton."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '10.1', 'title': "Booth's Multiplication Algorithm for Signed 2's Complement", 'content': "• <b>Why Booth's Algorithm?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Standard binary multiplication fails for negative signed numbers.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Booth's algorithm works directly on 2's complement numbers and speeds up multiplication by skipping runs of consecutive 1s.<br/><br/>• <b>The Decision Table (Inspect bit pair Q_0 and Q_{-1}):</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Q_0  Q_{-1}   Meaning             Action Taken<br/>--------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/> 0     0     Run of zeros        Only Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR)<br/> 0     1     End of run of 1s    Accumulator A = A + Multiplicand M, then ASR<br/> 1     0     Start of run of 1s  Accumulator A = A - Multiplicand M, then ASR<br/> 1     1     Run of ones         Only Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR)</div>• <b>Crucial: Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR) Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Shift all bits to the right by 1 position.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The Most Significant Bit (MSB, Sign Bit) is <b>REPLICATED</b> (kept identical to preserve the negative sign!).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Worst case for Booth's algorithm is alternating bits like 01010101 (requires n additions/subtractions). Best case is all 1s (11111111) or all 0s (only 1 addition/subtraction or 0)!"}, {'number': '10.2', 'title': 'Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Units', 'content': '• <b>Hardwired Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Built using logic gates, flip-flops, and decoders.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Pros:</b> Maximum possible speed. Used in high-performance RISC CPUs.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Cons:</b> Impossible to modify or add instructions without replacing physical chips.<br/><br/>• <b>Microprogrammed Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Control signals are stored as micro-instructions inside a Control Memory (ROM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Horizontal Microprogramming:</b> 1 bit per control signal. Fast, unencoded, but control words are very wide.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Vertical Microprogramming:</b> Signals are grouped into mutually exclusive fields. If a field has k signals, it requires <b>⌈log2(k + 1)⌉ bits</b> (the +1 is for the NO-OP inactive state!).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In vertical microprogramming, always add 1 for the inactive state before taking log2: Bits = ⌈log2(Number of Signals + 1)⌉!'}, {'number': '10.3', 'title': 'DMA (Direct Memory Access) Data Transfer & Cycle Stealing', 'content': '• <b>What is DMA?</b> Allows I/O devices (hard drives, NICs) to transfer data directly to/from RAM without routing through the CPU.<br/>• <b>Cycle Stealing Mode:</b> DMA controller takes control of the memory bus for 1 bus cycle to transfer 1 data word, then releases it back to CPU.<br/>• <b>Formula for Percentage CPU Slowdown:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>% CPU Time Stolen = (Device Transfer Rate / Bus Bandwidth) × 100%</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: If device prepares 1 byte every 4 µs and bus transfer takes 0.5 µs:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;CPU cycle stolen fraction = 0.5 µs / 4 µs = 12.5%.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DMA calculations, verify the units! Bus bandwidth and device preparation speed must be converted to the same time base (e.g. bytes per second) before dividing.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: DMA Cycle Stealing Percentage', 'problem': 'A hard disk transfers data at 2 MB/sec in cycle stealing mode. The system memory bus has a transfer speed of 10 MB/sec. What percentage of CPU cycle time is consumed by DMA?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Device data transfer rate = 2 MB/sec.', 'Step 2: Bus transfer bandwidth = 10 MB/sec.', 'Step 3: Percentage of CPU cycles stolen = (Device Rate / Bus Rate) × 100%.', 'Step 4: Calculation: (2 MB/s / 10 MB/s) × 100% = 0.2 × 100% = 20%.'], 'answer': 'CPU cycle time consumed = 20%.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In vertical microprogramming, forgetting the +1 for the NO-OP state is the #1 student mistake in GATE!', "In Booth's algorithm, shifting is ARITHMETIC shift right, never logical shift right (do NOT shift in a 0 on the left if the number is negative!)."]}, ('COA', 2): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Memory Hierarchy, Cache Mapping & AMAT', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Desk, Bookshelf & Public Library', 'text': '• Registers: The pen and paper right in your hands (0 nanoseconds).\n• Cache: The small bookshelf right beside your desk (a few seconds).\n• Main Memory (RAM): The library across the college campus (10 minutes).\n• Secondary Storage (Disk): Ordering a book by postal mail from another state (days)!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '11.1', 'title': 'Cache Address Partitioning (Direct, Associative, Set-Associative)', 'content': "• <b>Every memory address is partitioned into 3 fields:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Direct Mapped:       [ Tag Bits        | Line Bits (Index) | Block/Word Offset ]<br/>k-Way Set-Associative:[ Tag Bits        | Set Bits (Index)  | Block/Word Offset ]<br/>Fully Associative:   [ Tag Bits        | No Index Bits     | Block/Word Offset ]</div>• <b>Formulas to Calculate Field Widths:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Word / Byte Offset Bits:</b> <code>log2(Block Size in bytes)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Number of Cache Lines:</b> <code>Total Cache Size / Block Size</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Number of Sets:</b> <code>Total Cache Lines / k</code> (for k-way set associative).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Set Bits:</b> <code>log2(Number of Sets)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Tag Bits:</b> <code>Total Physical Address Bits - Set Bits - Word Offset Bits</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Tag Directory Size Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Tag Directory Size = (Total Cache Lines) × (Tag Bits + Valid Bit + Dirty Bit)</code>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Total cache capacity stores only DATA! The Tag Directory is extra hardware overhead: Tag Directory Size = (Number of Lines) × (Tag + 1 Valid + 1 Dirty bit)!'}, {'number': '11.2', 'title': 'Average Memory Access Time (AMAT) Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Model 1: Hierarchical Access (L1 checked first; on miss, lower level is visited):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = t_{hit_L1} + (Miss_Rate_L1 × Miss_Penalty_L1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If miss penalty goes to L2 cache: Miss_Penalty_L1 = t_{hit_L2} + (Miss_Rate_L2 × t_{mem}).<br/><br/>• <b>Model 2: Simultaneous Access (CPU queries Cache and Memory in parallel):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = (Hit_Rate × t_{cache}) + (Miss_Rate × t_{mem})</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Cache Write Policies:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Through:</b> Writes to both cache and RAM simultaneously. Safe, simple, but slow; needs Write Buffer.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Back:</b> Writes only to cache. RAM updated only when a modified block is evicted. Fast; requires <b>Dirty Bit</b>.', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unless an exam question explicitly says 'Simultaneous Access', ALWAYS use the Hierarchical formula where Miss Penalty includes access time to the next lower level!"}, {'number': '11.3', 'title': 'The 3 Cs of Cache Misses', 'content': '• <b>1. Compulsory Miss (Cold Start):</b> First-ever access to a block. Unavoidable regardless of cache size.<br/>• <b>2. Capacity Miss:</b> Cache is physically too small to hold all active working set blocks. Occurs even in Fully Associative caches.<br/>• <b>3. Conflict Miss (Collision):</b> Multiple memory blocks map to the same set/line even though empty lines exist elsewhere.<br/>• <b>Key Insight:</b> Fully Associative cache has <b>ZERO conflict misses</b>!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Increasing associativity (e.g. 2-way to 4-way) reduces Conflict misses, but increases hit time because the tag comparator logic becomes wider and more complex.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Full Cache Address Splitting', 'problem': 'A 32-bit byte-addressable system has a 64 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. Find Tag bits, Set bits, Word offset bits, and Tag directory size (assume 1 valid bit).', 'steps': ['Step 1: Word offset = log2(32 bytes) = 5 bits.', 'Step 2: Total lines = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048 lines.', 'Step 3: Number of sets = 2048 lines / 4 lines per set = 512 sets.', 'Step 4: Set index bits = log2(512) = 9 bits.', 'Step 5: Tag bits = 32 - (9 + 5) = 32 - 14 = 18 bits.', 'Step 6: Tag directory size = 2048 lines × (18 tag bits + 1 valid bit) = 2048 × 19 bits = 38,912 bits = 4864 bytes.'], 'answer': 'Tag = 18 bits, Set = 9 bits, Offset = 5 bits, Tag Directory = 4864 bytes.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In word-addressable systems, word offset bits depend on word size (e.g. if 1 word = 4 bytes, 32-byte block holds 8 words ⇒ offset is 3 bits, not 5 bits!).', 'Direct-mapped cache is simply a 1-way set-associative cache; fully associative cache has exactly 1 set.']}, ('COA', 3): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Pipeline Hazards, Speedup & Instruction Level Parallelism', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Laundromat Assembly Line', 'text': '• Non-Pipelined: You wash load 1 (30 min), dry (30 min), fold (30 min), iron (30 min). Total = 2 hours per load. 4 loads take 8 hours!\n• Pipelined: As soon as load 1 finishes washing and enters the dryer, you drop load 2 into the washer! After a warm-up period, one complete load finishes every 30 minutes! Throughput increases 4x.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '12.1', 'title': 'The 5-Stage Classic RISC Pipeline & Clock Cycle', 'content': '• <b>The 5 Pipeline Stages:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>IF (Instruction Fetch):</b> Read instruction from Instruction Cache into IF/ID register.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>ID (Instruction Decode / Register Read):</b> Decode opcode and read source register operands.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>EX (Execute / Address Calculation):</b> ALU performs operation or computes effective memory address.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>MEM (Memory Access):</b> Read or write data from/to Data Cache (for LOAD/STORE).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>WB (Write Back):</b> Write computed result back into destination register.<br/><br/>• <b>Clock Cycle Period Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Clock Period (τ) = max(Stage Delays) + Pipeline Register Delay</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;The slowest stage sets the speed for all stages!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Pipeline clock cycle cannot be faster than the slowest stage! If stages take 100ps, 150ps, 250ps, 120ps, 110ps with 20ps buffer, Clock Period = 250 + 20 = 270ps.'}, {'number': '12.2', 'title': 'The Three Classic Pipeline Hazards & Solutions', 'content': '• <b>1. Structural Hazard (Hardware Resource Conflict):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Two instructions need the same hardware simultaneously (e.g. IF fetching instruction while MEM reads data from single-port RAM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> Separate Instruction Cache & Data Cache (Harvard Architecture).<br/><br/>• <b>2. Data Hazard (RAW - Read After Write):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Instruction 2 needs register result calculated by Instruction 1 before Inst 1 writes it back in WB stage.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> <b>Operand Forwarding (Bypassing)</b> feeds result directly from EX/MEM output to EX input without waiting for WB!<br/><br/>• <b>3. Control Hazard (Branch Instructions):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Target address not known until ID or EX stage, causing wrong instructions to be fetched.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> Branch Prediction, Branch Target Buffer, Delayed Branching.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In standard in-order pipelining, only RAW (True Dependency) causes pipeline stalls! WAR and WAW hazards only occur in out-of-order processors.'}, {'number': '12.3', 'title': 'Pipeline Speedup & Effective CPI', 'content': '• <b>Execution Time:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Non-pipelined time for n instructions: <code>T_nonpipe = n × k × τ</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Pipelined time for n instructions: <code>T_pipe = (k + n - 1 + Stalls) × τ</code>.<br/>• <b>Speedup (S):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Speedup = T_nonpipe / T_pipe</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For large n (n → ∞) with no stalls: <b>Maximum Ideal Speedup = k</b> (number of stages!).<br/>• <b>Effective CPI:</b> <code>CPI = 1 + (Stalls per instruction)</code>. Actual Speedup = <code>k / CPI</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If 20% of instructions are branch instructions and each branch causes 2 stall cycles: Effective CPI = 1 + (0.20 × 2) = 1.4! Speedup over non-pipelined = k / 1.4.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Pipeline Speedup Calculation', 'problem': 'A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 2 ns, 1.5 ns, 3 ns, 2 ns, 1 ns. Register delay is 0.5 ns. In non-pipelined mode, an instruction takes 10 ns. Calculate the clock period and maximum speedup for a large number of instructions without stalls.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Clock period τ = max(2, 1.5, 3, 2, 1) + 0.5 = 3 + 0.5 = 3.5 ns.', 'Step 2: Non-pipelined time per instruction = 10 ns.', 'Step 3: Pipelined time per instruction for large n (ideal CPI = 1) = 1 × 3.5 ns = 3.5 ns.', 'Step 4: Speedup S = 10 ns / 3.5 ns = 2.857.'], 'answer': 'Clock period = 3.5 ns; Speedup = 2.86x.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Speedup is k ONLY when all stage delays are perfectly identical and register delay is 0!', 'Even with operand forwarding, a LOAD instruction immediately followed by an ALU instruction that uses the loaded value still requires 1 STALL cycle (Load-Use data hazard)!']}, ('PDS', 0): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Programming in C & Recursion Tracing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Russian Matryoshka Doll (Recursion)', 'text': '• Recursion: Opening a nesting doll to find a smaller doll inside. You keep unpacking until you hit the smallest solid doll (Base Case). Then you close them all back together (Call Stack unwinding)!\n• Array Decay: Handing someone a business card with your address written on it instead of physically moving your whole house to them.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '13.1', 'title': 'Pointers & Array Equivalence in C (Deep Tutorial)', 'content': "• <b>1. Visualizing Memory: How an Array is Stored in C:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Suppose you write: <code>int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};</code> (assuming 4 bytes per integer):<br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Index:      arr[0]      arr[1]      arr[2]      arr[3]<br/>Value:        10          20          30          40<br/>Address:    [1000]      [1004]      [1008]      [1012]<br/>               ↑<br/>            arr (points here)</div>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The array name <code>arr</code> is a constant pointer:</b> It points to the first element: <code>arr == &arr[0] == 1000</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Because <code>arr</code> is a constant pointer, you <b>cannot reassign it</b> (<code>arr++</code> causes a compilation error!).<br/><br/>• <b>2. The Subscript Rule: Why does <code>3[arr] == arr[3]</code> work?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• In C, square brackets <code>[]</code> do not exist in machine code! C compiler translates <code>arr[i]</code> into:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>arr[i] ≡ *(arr + i)</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Pointer addition automatically scales by type size: <code>Address = Base + (i × sizeof(type))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Since addition is commutative (<code>a + b == b + a</code>), <code>*(arr + i)</code> is identical to <code>*(i + arr)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Therefore, in C: <code>arr[i] ≡ *(arr + i) ≡ *(i + arr) ≡ i[arr]</code>! Writing <code>3[arr]</code> gives 40!<br/><br/>• <b>3. Pointer Increments: <code>*p++</code> vs <code>(*p)++</code> vs <code>*++p</code>:</b><br/><div class='space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm my-2'>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>*p++</code>:</b> Reads <code>*p</code> value first, then increments pointer <code>p</code> to point to next integer.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>(*p)++</code>:</b> Pointer <code>p</code> does NOT move! Increments the actual integer in memory by 1 (e.g. 10 becomes 11).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>*++p</code>:</b> Advances pointer <code>p</code> to next slot first, then dereferences that new slot.</div>• <b>4. The Giant Trap: <code>arr + 1</code> vs <code>&arr + 1</code>:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>arr + 1</code> jumps <b>1 element (4 bytes)</b> ⇒ 1004.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>&arr + 1</code> jumps the <b>entire array of 4 ints (16 bytes)</b> ⇒ 1016!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When an array is passed into a function (like solve(arr)), it decays into a simple pointer! Inside the function, sizeof(arr) gives the size of a pointer (4 or 8 bytes), NEVER the array size!'}, {'number': '13.2', 'title': 'Short-Circuit Evaluation & Side Effects in C', 'content': '• <b>What is Short-Circuiting?</b> C guarantees left-to-right evaluation for logical operators and stops as soon as truth value is known.<br/>• <b>1. Logical AND (expr1 && expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 == 0 (False)</code>, the whole result MUST be False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 is NEVER EXECUTED!</b> Any increments or assignments inside expr2 are completely skipped.<br/>• <b>2. Logical OR (expr1 || expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 != 0 (True)</code>, the whole result MUST be True.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 is NEVER EXECUTED!</b><br/><br/>• <b>GATE Code Trace Example:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-amber-300 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>int a = 1, b = 0;<br/>int res = (a-- || ++b);<br/>printf("%d %d %d", a, b, res);<br/>// Trace: a-- evaluates to 1 (True), then decrements a to 0.<br/>// Because 1 is True, || short-circuits! ++b is NEVER touched!<br/>// Output: 0 0 1</div>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In short-circuit expressions, watch out for skipped side effects! In (x || y++), if x evaluates to true, y++ is NOT executed, and y remains unchanged!'}, {'number': '13.3', 'title': 'Storage Classes: static, auto, register, extern', 'content': '• <b>1. auto:</b> Default for local variables. Stored on Call Stack. Created when block entered, destroyed when exited.<br/>• <b>2. static:</b> Stored in Data Segment (not stack!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Initialized only ONCE</b> when program loads (defaults to 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Retains its value across multiple recursive calls or function invocations.<br/>• <b>3. register:</b> Request to keep variable in CPU register. Address-of operator <code>&var</code> is ILLEGAL.<br/>• <b>4. extern:</b> References a global variable defined in another file without allocating new storage.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In recursion questions with static int count = 0;, the line static int count = 0; is executed ONLY ONCE on the very first call. On all subsequent recursive calls, it is skipped!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Tracing Pointer Expressions', 'problem': 'Given: int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40}; int *p = a; What does *p++, (*p)++, *++p evaluate to step-by-step?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Pointer p starts at a[0] (value 10, address 1000).', 'Step 2: Execute *p++: Reads 10, then pointer p advances to a[1] (address 1004).', 'Step 3: Execute (*p)++: p points to a[1]. Reads 20, then increments the value inside a[1] from 20 to 21. p remains at a[1].', 'Step 4: Execute *++p: Pointer p advances to a[2] (address 1008) first, then reads value 30.', 'Step 5: Array is now {10, 21, 30, 40} and p points to a[2].'], 'answer': 'Final array = {10, 21, 30, 40}; pointer p points to a[2] (30).'}], 'exam_traps': ["In C, sizeof('a') is sizeof(int) = 4 bytes! In C++, sizeof('a') is 1 byte (char).", 'Array boundary overflow in C does NOT throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException! It results in undefined behavior (silent memory corruption).']}, ('PDS', 1): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Linear Data Structures - Stacks, Queues & Linked Lists', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Plate Dispenser & Ticket Counter', 'text': '• Stack (LIFO): The spring-loaded plate stack in a cafeteria. The last plate placed on top is the first one taken by a customer.\n• Queue (FIFO): A movie ticket line. The first person to arrive is the first person served.\n• Circular Queue: Like seats on a Ferris wheel—when the last seat passes, the wheel circles back to seat #1.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '14.1', 'title': 'Stack Infix to Postfix Conversion & Evaluation', 'content': '• <b>Why Postfix (Reverse Polish Notation)?</b> Computers evaluate postfix expressions in 1 linear pass without parentheses or backtracking.<br/>• <b>Postfix Evaluation Algorithm:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Scan expression from left to right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If operand (number): Push onto stack.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If operator: Pop two operands: <code>op2 = pop()</code> (right operand), <code>op1 = pop()</code> (left operand).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Calculate: <code>result = op1 (operator) op2</code>. Push result back onto stack.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of Valid Stack Permutations (Catalan Numbers):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total permutations obtainable using push/pop on n distinct elements:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Values: C_1 = 1, C_2 = 2, C_3 = 5, C_4 = 14, C_5 = 42.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In postfix evaluation, the FIRST popped element is the RIGHT operand, and the SECOND popped element is the LEFT operand: op1 - op2 (order matters for subtraction and division)!'}, {'number': '14.2', 'title': 'Circular Queue Boundary Conditions', 'content': '• In a linear array queue, dequeuing leaves dead space at the front that cannot be reused without shifting.<br/>• <b>Circular Queue Modulo Operations (Array Size N):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Enqueue(x):</b> <code>rear = (rear + 1) % N; queue[rear] = x;</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dequeue():</b> <code>front = (front + 1) % N; val = queue[front];</code><br/>• <b>Full Condition:</b> <code>(rear + 1) % N == front</code> (leaves 1 empty slot to distinguish from empty!).<br/>• <b>Empty Condition:</b> <code>front == rear</code>.<br/>• <b>Current Element Count:</b> <code>(rear - front + N) % N</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a circular queue of array size N that leaves 1 slot empty, the maximum capacity is (N - 1) elements!'}, {'number': '14.3', 'title': "Linked Lists & Floyd's Cycle Detection", 'content': "• <b>Floyd's Tortoise and Hare Cycle Finding:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Slow pointer moves 1 node per step; Fast pointer moves 2 nodes per step.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If a cycle exists, Fast will catch up and meet Slow inside the loop in O(N) time and O(1) space!<br/>• <b>Finding the Starting Node of the Cycle:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Once Slow and Fast meet, keep Fast at the meeting point.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Reset Slow to the <code>head</code> of the list.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Advance both pointers 1 step at a time. The node where they meet is the exact cycle entry point!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Reversing a singly linked list in-place requires O(N) time and strictly O(1) auxiliary space using 3 pointers: prev, curr, and next.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Evaluating Postfix with Stack', 'problem': 'Evaluate the postfix expression: 12 4 / 3 * 7 2 - +', 'steps': ['Step 1: Push 12, Push 4. Stack: [12, 4].', "Step 2: '/' arrives: op2 = 4, op1 = 12. Compute 12 / 4 = 3. Push 3. Stack: [3].", 'Step 3: Push 3. Stack: [3, 3].', "Step 4: '*' arrives: op2 = 3, op1 = 3. Compute 3 * 3 = 9. Push 9. Stack: [9].", 'Step 5: Push 7, Push 2. Stack: [9, 7, 2].', "Step 6: '-' arrives: op2 = 2, op1 = 7. Compute 7 - 2 = 5. Push 5. Stack: [9, 5].", "Step 7: '+' arrives: op2 = 5, op1 = 9. Compute 9 + 5 = 14. Push 14. Stack: [14]."], 'answer': 'Final evaluated value = 14.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In postfix conversion, operators with higher or EQUAL precedence on the stack must be popped first for left-associative operators!', 'In queue implemented using two stacks, amortized time per operation is O(1), but worst-case single dequeue can take O(N).']}, ('PDS', 2): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Trees, Binary Search Trees & Binary Heaps', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Corporate Hierarchy & Emergency Room Triage', 'text': '• Binary Tree: Like a management chart where every manager has at most two direct reports (left and right vice presidents).\n• Priority Queue (Heap): Like an emergency room triage where doctors treat patients by severity of injury (priority), not arrival order!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '15.1', 'title': 'Binary Tree Mathematical Properties & Traversals', 'content': '• <b>Key Mathematical Theorems for Binary Trees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Leaf vs Degree-2 Nodes:</b> In any binary tree: <b>n_0 = n_2 + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Number of leaf nodes is ALWAYS 1 more than the number of nodes with 2 children!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Max nodes at height h (root at h=0):</b> <b>2^(h + 1) - 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Strictly Binary Tree with n internal nodes:</b> Has exactly <b>2n edges</b> and <b>n + 1 leaves</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Tree Reconstruction from Traversals:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Preorder + Inorder:</b> Produces a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Postorder + Inorder:</b> Produces a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Preorder + Postorder:</b> CANNOT uniquely construct a general binary tree!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Inorder traversal MUST be given to reconstruct a unique binary tree! Without Inorder, left and right subtrees cannot be separated.'}, {'number': '15.2', 'title': 'Binary Search Trees (BST) & AVL Balance Rotations', 'content': '• <b>BST Inorder Property:</b> Inorder traversal of ANY valid BST ALWAYS yields keys in strictly <b>ASCENDING SORTED ORDER</b>.<br/>• <b>Number of distinct BSTs with n distinct keys:</b> Catalan Number <b>C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>• <b>AVL Trees (Self-Balancing BST):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Balance Factor = <code>Height(Left Subtree) - Height(Right Subtree) ∈ {-1, 0, +1}</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Balance Factor becomes ±2, rebalance via 4 rotations:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LL:</b> Single right rotation.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RR:</b> Single left rotation.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LR:</b> Double rotation (Left on child, Right on root).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RL:</b> Double rotation (Right on child, Left on root).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In any BST, the node with maximum value has NO right child, and the node with minimum value has NO left child!'}, {'number': '15.3', 'title': 'Binary Heaps & Build-Heap O(n) Complexity', 'content': '• <b>Array Representation (1-based indexing for node at index i):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parent = <code>⌊i / 2⌋</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Left Child = <code>2i</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Right Child = <code>2i + 1</code>.<br/>• <b>Heap Complexities:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Find Min/Max: <b>O(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Insert key: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Extract Min/Max: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Build-Heap (from arbitrary array):</b> <b>O(n) linear time!</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Why O(n)? Most nodes are near leaves with tiny heights: ∑ (h / 2^h) converges to 2.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Building a binary heap takes O(n) time, NOT O(n log n)! In a Min-Heap of n elements, the maximum element is always at one of the leaf nodes (indices ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 to n).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Reconstructing Binary Tree', 'problem': 'Given Preorder: [A, B, D, E, C, F] and Inorder: [D, B, E, A, F, C]. What is the Postorder traversal?', 'steps': ['Step 1: First element in Preorder is Root = A.', 'Step 2: Find A in Inorder: Left subtree is {D, B, E}; Right subtree is {F, C}.', 'Step 3: In Preorder, next 3 items {B, D, E} belong to Left subtree ⇒ B is root of Left subtree.', 'Step 4: In Inorder, D is left of B, E is right of B.', 'Step 5: For Right subtree: Preorder has C then F; Inorder has F before C ⇒ C is root, F is left child.', 'Step 6: Compute Postorder (Left, Right, Root): D, E, B, F, C, A.'], 'answer': 'Postorder: D, E, B, F, C, A.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In a Max-Heap with n elements, finding the minimum element takes O(n) time, because you must scan all leaf nodes!', 'Deleting an arbitrary element from a heap takes O(log n) time provided its index is known; finding it takes O(n).']}, ('ALGO', 0): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Asymptotic Analysis, Master Theorem & Recurrences', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Delivery Speed of Transport Modes', 'text': "• Big-O: An upper bound promise. Like saying 'a flight from Delhi to Mumbai takes at most 3 hours'. It might take 2 hours, but will never exceed 3 hours.\n• Big-Omega (Ω): A lower bound guarantee. 'Walking from Delhi to Mumbai will take at least 5 days'.\n• Big-Theta (Θ): A tight bound. Both upper and lower bounds match."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '16.1', 'title': 'Asymptotic Growth Hierarchy & Limits', 'content': "• <b>Standard Rate of Growth Hierarchy (Memorize for GATE!):</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-yellow-300 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>c < log(log n) < log n < (log n)² < √n < n < n log n < n² < n³ < 2^n < 3^n < n! < n^n</div>• <b>Testing Growth Rates with Limits:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compute <code>L = lim_{n→∞} [ f(n) / g(n) ]</code>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = 0: f(n) grows strictly slower: <code>f(n) = o(g(n))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = constant (0 < L < ∞): <code>f(n) = Θ(g(n))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = ∞: f(n) grows strictly faster: <code>f(n) = ω(g(n))</code>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Polynomials always beat logarithms: (log n)^1000 = o(n^0.001) for sufficiently large n! Exponentials always beat polynomials: n^1000 = o(1.001^n)!'}, {'number': '16.2', 'title': 'Master Theorem for Divide-and-Conquer Recurrences', 'content': '• For recurrence <b>T(n) = a · T(n / b) + Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b> (where a ≥ 1, b > 1):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compare <b>a</b> with <b>b^k</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Case 1 (a > b^k):</b> Root work dominates ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Case 2 (a = b^k):</b> Work balanced across levels:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p > -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^{p+1} n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p = -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log(log n))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Case 3 (a < b^k):</b> Work at leaf level dominates ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n/log n: a = 2, b = 2, k = 1 ⇒ a = b^k, but p = -1! By Case 2 (p = -1): T(n) = Θ(n log(log n))!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Applying Master Theorem', 'problem': 'Solve T(n) = 8 T(n / 2) + n² log² n.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify: a = 8, b = 2, f(n) = n² log² n ⇒ k = 2, p = 2.', 'Step 2: Calculate b^k = 2² = 4.', 'Step 3: Compare a with b^k: a = 8 > 4 = b^k ⇒ Case 1!', 'Step 4: Exponent log_b a = log_2 8 = 3.', 'Step 5: Therefore, T(n) = Θ(n^3).'], 'answer': 'T(n) = Θ(n³).'}], 'exam_traps': ['Master Theorem cannot be applied if a is not constant (e.g. 2^n T(n/2)) or if b is not constant!', 'Master theorem for subtracting recurrences: T(n) = T(n - 1) + n gives Θ(n²); T(n) = T(n - 1) + 1 gives Θ(n).']}, ('ALGO', 1): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Sorting Algorithms, Lower Bounds & Hash Tables', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Playing Card Hand & Hotel Keycard Racks', 'text': '• Insertion Sort: Like sorting cards in your hand—you pick each new card and slide it into its correct position among already sorted cards.\n• Hash Table: Like a hotel keycard rack. The room number directly maps to the hook without searching through all keys.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '17.1', 'title': 'Master Summary Table of Sorting Algorithms', 'content': "• <b>Complete Comparison Table for GATE CSE:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Algorithm      Best Time     Avg Time      Worst Time    Space     Stable?   Method<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Insertion Sort O(n)          O(n²)         O(n²)         O(1)      YES       Incremental<br/>Merge Sort     O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n)      YES       Divide & Conquer<br/>Heap Sort      O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(1)      NO        Selection/Heap<br/>Quick Sort     O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n²)         O(log n)  NO        Partitioning<br/>Counting Sort  O(n + k)      O(n + k)      O(n + k)      O(k)      YES       Non-comparison</div>• <b>Stability Definition:</b> Preserves the relative order of records with equal keys.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The theoretical lower bound for any comparison-based sorting algorithm in the worst case is Ω(n log n)! Non-comparison sorts (Counting, Radix) achieve O(n) by using array indexing instead of comparisons.'}, {'number': '17.2', 'title': 'Hash Table Collisions: Chaining vs Open Addressing', 'content': "• <b>Separate Chaining:</b> Each slot holds a linked list. Load factor α can exceed 1.<br/>• <b>Open Addressing (All keys stored in table, α ≤ 1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Linear Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + i) % m</code>. Suffers from <b>Primary Clustering</b> (long blocks of occupied slots).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Quadratic Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + c1·i + c2·i²) % m</code>. Eliminates primary clustering; suffers from <b>Secondary Clustering</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Double Hashing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i · h2(k)) % m</code>. Eliminates both clusterings (h2(k) must be relatively prime to m).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In open addressing, expected number of probes for unsuccessful search = 1 / (1 - α); for successful search = (1 / α) ln(1 / (1 - α))!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Double Hashing Probe Sequence', 'problem': 'Insert key 75 into a table of size 11 using h1(k) = k % 11 and h2(k) = 1 + (k % 10). If slot h1(75) is occupied, find the next probe slot.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Primary hash h1(75) = 75 % 11 = 9.', 'Step 2: Slot 9 is occupied ⇒ collision!', 'Step 3: Secondary hash h2(75) = 1 + (75 % 10) = 1 + 5 = 6.', 'Step 4: Next probe for i = 1: (h1(k) + 1 × h2(k)) % 11 = (9 + 6) % 11 = 15 % 11 = 4.'], 'answer': 'Next probe slot is index 4.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In QuickSort, if the array is ALREADY SORTED and the pivot is chosen as first or last element, QuickSort degrades to its WORST-CASE O(n²) time!', 'In hashing with open addressing, deleted slots must be marked with a special DELETED marker; setting to NULL breaks probe chains.']}, ('ALGO', 2): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Greedy Algorithms, Dynamic Programming & Divide-and-Conquer', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Making Change & Remembering Past Math Results', 'text': "• Greedy: Like a cashier giving change with the fewest coins by picking the largest coin possible first (25c, then 10c, etc.).\n• Dynamic Programming: 'What is 1 + 1 + 1 + 1? 4! Now add another + 1? 5!' You didn't re-count 1 to 4 because you REMEMBERED the previous result (Memoization)!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '18.1', 'title': 'Greedy Method vs Dynamic Programming', 'content': "• <b>Greedy Method:</b> Makes locally optimal choice at each step without backtracking.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: Fractional Knapsack, Huffman Coding, Kruskal's, Prim's, Dijkstra.<br/>• <b>Dynamic Programming (DP):</b> Explores all possibilities by breaking into subproblems, but solves each subproblem only once.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Requires <b>Optimal Substructure</b> AND <b>Overlapping Subproblems</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: 0/1 Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence (LCS), Matrix Chain Multiplication, Floyd-Warshall.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fractional Knapsack is solved by Greedy (sort by value/weight in O(n log n)). 0/1 Knapsack CANNOT be solved by Greedy; it requires Dynamic Programming in O(n × W) pseudo-polynomial time!'}, {'number': '18.2', 'title': 'Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) Recurrence', 'content': '• For strings X of length m and Y of length n:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] == Y[j]</b>: <code>LCS[i, j] = 1 + LCS[i - 1, j - 1]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] ≠ Y[j]</b>: <code>LCS[i, j] = max(LCS[i - 1, j], LCS[i, j - 1])</code>.<br/>• <b>Time & Space:</b> O(m × n) time; space can be optimized to O(min(m, n)) using only two rows.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Matrix Chain Multiplication with n matrices, number of different parenthesizations is given by Catalan Number C_{n-1}!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Huffman Code Bit Length', 'problem': 'Frequencies: A: 45, B: 13, C: 12, D: 16, E: 9, F: 5. Construct Huffman tree and find total bits required.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Merge smallest two: F(5) + E(9) = 14.', 'Step 2: Merge next two smallest: C(12) + B(13) = 25.', 'Step 3: Merge 14 and D(16) = 30.', 'Step 4: Merge 25 and 30 = 55.', 'Step 5: Merge A(45) and 55 = 100.', 'Step 6: Code lengths: A = 1 bit; B, C, D = 3 bits; E, F = 4 bits.', 'Step 7: Total bits = (45×1) + (13×3) + (12×3) + (16×3) + (9×4) + (5×4) = 45 + 39 + 36 + 48 + 36 + 20 = 224 bits.'], 'answer': 'Total encoded length = 224 bits.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Huffman code is a prefix code: No codeword is a prefix of another!', 'Huffman tree is always a strictly full binary tree with 2n - 1 total nodes for n characters.']}, ('ALGO', 3): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Graph Traversals, MST & Shortest Paths', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: GPS Navigation & Fiber-Optic City Network', 'text': "• Dijkstra's Algorithm: How Google Maps finds the fastest driving route to your destination.\n• Minimum Spanning Tree: Laying down high-speed fiber cables between cities with the minimum total cost while ensuring every city is connected."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '19.1', 'title': 'BFS vs DFS Comparison & Applications', 'content': "• <b>Breadth-First Search (BFS):</b> Uses a <b>Queue</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds the <b>shortest path in unweighted graphs</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects bipartiteness (2-coloring).<br/>• <b>Depth-First Search (DFS):</b> Uses a <b>Stack / Recursion</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects cycles in directed graphs (via <b>Back Edges</b>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Computes <b>Topological Sort</b> of a DAG (reverse of finish times).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's / Tarjan's algorithm).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DFS of a directed graph, a cycle exists IF AND ONLY IF there is at least one BACK EDGE! Forward and Cross edges do NOT indicate a cycle.'}, {'number': '19.2', 'title': 'Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford vs Floyd-Warshall', 'content': "• <b>Dijkstra's Algorithm:</b> Single-Source Shortest Path for <b>non-negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <b>O((V + E) log V)</b> using min-heap. FAILS for negative edge weights!<br/>• <b>Bellman-Ford Algorithm:</b> Works with <b>negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relaxes all edges (V - 1) times. Time: <b>O(V × E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects negative-weight cycles (if distance decreases on V-th relaxation).<br/>• <b>Floyd-Warshall Algorithm:</b> All-Pairs Shortest Path in <b>O(V³)</b> time using dynamic programming.<br/>• <b>Kruskal vs Prim (MST):</b> Kruskal uses edges (O(E log E) with DSU); Prim grows from a vertex (O(E + V log V)).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If all edge weights in a graph are distinct, the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) is 100% UNIQUE! Shortest path tree is NOT necessarily unique.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Kruskal's MST Edge Selection", 'problem': 'Given edges sorted by weight: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (A-C, 2), (C-D, 3), (B-D, 4). Which edges are included in the MST?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Pick (A-B, 1) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 2: Pick (B-C, 2) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 3: Consider (A-C, 2) ⇒ A, B, C are already connected! Adding A-C creates cycle A-B-C-A. REJECT.', 'Step 4: Pick (C-D, 3) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 5: We have 4 vertices and 3 edges (V - 1 = 3). MST is complete!'], 'answer': 'MST edges: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (C-D, 3). Total weight = 6.'}], 'exam_traps': ["Dijkstra's algorithm can enter an infinite loop or give wrong answers if negative edge weights are present!", 'Topological sort exists IF AND ONLY IF the graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).']}, ('TOC', 0): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Finite Automata, Regular Expressions & Minimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Soda Vending Machine State Controller', 'text': "• Finite Automata: Like a soda machine. Inserting a coin transitions it from state 'Empty' to 'Has 50c'. Pressing the button dispenses soda and resets to 'Empty'. It has finite memory—it only remembers its current state!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '20.1', 'title': 'DFA vs NFA: Equivalences, Power & State Bounds', 'content': '• <b>Equivalent Computational Power:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Every NFA can be converted into an equivalent DFA using <b>Subset Construction</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If an NFA has <b>n states</b>, the equivalent minimized DFA has at most <b>2^n states</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Key Distinctions in Transitions:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. In DFA: For every state and input symbol, there is <b>exactly ONE</b> deterministic transition: <code>δ: Q × Σ → Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. In NFA: There can be 0, 1, or multiple transitions, plus ε-transitions: <code>δ: Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) → 2^Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Dead / Trap States:</b> DFA requires explicit trap states for invalid input prefixes; NFA simply drops the branch.<br/><br/>• <b>State Minimization:</b> Done via Myhill-Nerode Theorem / Table-Filling Algorithm by grouping 0-equivalent, 1-equivalent, ..., k-equivalent states.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: DFA, NFA, and ε-NFA all have EQUAL computational power! They all recognize the exact same family: Regular Languages. Minimizing a DFA always produces a 100% UNIQUE minimal DFA!'}, {'number': '20.2', 'title': "Regular Expressions & Arden's Theorem", 'content': "• <b>Arden's Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If P and Q are regular expressions and P does NOT contain ε, then the equation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>R = Q + RP</b> has a <b>UNIQUE solution: R = QP*</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Identities to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>(r*)* = r*</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>(r + s)* = (r* · s*)* = (r* + s*)*</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>r · (s · r)* = (r · s)* · r</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>ε + r · r* = r* = ε + r* · r</code>", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Arden's theorem R = Q + RP, P is on the RIGHT of R, giving R = QP*! If R = Q + PR (left recursion), the solution is P*Q."}, {'number': '20.3', 'title': 'Closure Properties of Regular Languages', 'content': "• <b>Regular Languages are CLOSED under ALL standard operations:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Union:</b> L1 ∪ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L' is Regular (swap final and non-final states in a complete DFA!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Difference:</b> L1 - L2 = L1 ∩ L2' is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Concatenation:</b> L1 · L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Kleene Star:</b> L* is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Reversal:</b> L^R is Regular.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regular languages are closed under ALL standard boolean operations! To complement a DFA, swap final and non-final states (make sure the DFA is complete with trap states first!).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Minimum DFA States for Suffix '01'", 'problem': "Find the minimum number of states in a DFA accepting all binary strings ending with substring '01'.", 'steps': ["Step 1: Length of suffix pattern '01' is 2.", 'Step 2: Formula: For strings ending with a specific pattern of length k, minimum DFA states = k + 1.', 'Step 3: States = 2 + 1 = 3: q0 (start/empty), q1 (last bit 0), q2 (last bits 01 - Accept).', "Step 4: Transitions from q2: on '0' go to q1; on '1' go to q0."], 'answer': 'Minimum states = 3.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Swapping final and non-final states on an NFA does NOT complement the language! You must convert the NFA to a complete DFA first.', 'Pumping Lemma can prove a language is NOT regular; it can NEVER prove a language IS regular.']}, ('TOC', 1): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Context-Free Grammars, Pushdown Automata & Chomsky Hierarchy', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Balancing Scale & Parenthesis Matcher', 'text': '• PDA Stack: Like checking code syntax for matching curly braces {}. When { arrives, push onto stack. When } arrives, pop off stack. If stack is empty at end, code syntax is balanced!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '21.1', 'title': 'Deterministic PDA (DPDA) vs Non-Deterministic PDA (NPDA)', 'content': "• <b>Crucial Distinction:</b> NPDA is <b>strictly more powerful</b> than DPDA!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- DPDA accepts <b>Deterministic Context-Free Languages (DCFL)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- NPDA accepts <b>Context-Free Languages (CFL)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Canonical Language Examples for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <code>L = { w c w^R }</code> has a marker 'c' ⇒ <b>DCFL</b> (accepted by DPDA).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <code>L = { w w^R }</code> has NO marker (even palindrome) ⇒ <b>CFL, but NOT DCFL</b> (requires non-deterministic guess of midpoint!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <code>L = { a^n b^n c^n }</code> ⇒ <b>NOT CFL</b> (requires 2 stacks = Turing Machine).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unlike Finite Automata where DFA == NFA, in Pushdown Automata NPDA is STRICTLY MORE POWERFUL than DPDA: DCFL ⊂ CFL!'}, {'number': '21.2', 'title': 'CFL Closure Properties (The Famous Exceptions!)', 'content': "• <b>CFLs are CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Union, Concatenation, Kleene Star, Reversal.<br/>• <b>CFLs are NOT CLOSED under (Frequently tested in GATE!):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L' is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>• <b>Intersection with Regular Language:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If L is CFL and R is Regular, then <b>L ∩ R is ALWAYS CFL</b>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: CFL is NOT closed under Intersection or Complement! But DCFL is CLOSED under Complement (though NOT closed under Union or Intersection).'}, {'number': '21.3', 'title': 'The Chomsky Hierarchy of Languages', 'content': '• <b>Type 3 (Regular):</b> Accepted by Finite Automata. Production: A → aB or A → a.<br/>• <b>Type 2 (Context-Free):</b> Accepted by Pushdown Automata. Production: A → α.<br/>• <b>Type 1 (Context-Sensitive):</b> Accepted by Linear Bounded Automata (LBA). Production: α → β where |α| ≤ |β|.<br/>• <b>Type 0 (Unrestricted / Recursively Enumerable):</b> Accepted by Turing Machine. Production: α → β.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strict hierarchy: Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ Recursive ⊂ Recursively Enumerable!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Intersection of Two CFLs', 'problem': 'L1 = { a^n b^n c^m | n, m ≥ 1 } and L2 = { a^m b^n c^n | m, n ≥ 1 }. Are L1 and L2 CFLs? Is L1 ∩ L2 a CFL?', 'steps': ["Step 1: L1 requires matching a's with b's (1 stack can do this) ⇒ L1 is CFL.", "Step 2: L2 requires matching b's with c's (1 stack can do this) ⇒ L2 is CFL.", 'Step 3: Intersection L1 ∩ L2 = { a^n b^n c^n | n ≥ 1 }.', 'Step 4: Checking 3 counts simultaneously requires 2 stacks (Turing machine power).', 'Step 5: Therefore, L1 ∩ L2 is NOT a CFL!'], 'answer': 'L1 and L2 are CFLs, but their intersection is NOT a CFL.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Inherent ambiguity: Some CFLs are inherently ambiguous (every grammar for them is ambiguous, e.g. {a^i b^j c^k | i=j or j=k}).', 'DCFLs are NEVER ambiguous! Every DCFL has an unambiguous grammar.']}, ('TOC', 2): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': "Turing Machines, Decidability & Rice's Theorem", 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Universal Antivirus Software Impossibility', 'text': '• Halting Problem: Can anyone write an antivirus software that inspects any arbitrary code and guarantees whether it will eventually terminate or freeze in an infinite loop? Alan Turing proved it is mathematically IMPOSSIBLE for all programs!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '22.1', 'title': 'Recursive (REC) vs Recursively Enumerable (RE)', 'content': "• <b>Recursive Language (REC / Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Turing Machine halts on EVERY input (Halts and Accepts if w ∈ L; Halts and Rejects if w ∉ L).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Closed under ALL operations including Complement</b>: If L is REC, L' is REC.<br/><br/>• <b>Recursively Enumerable Language (RE / Semi-Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Halts and accepts if w ∈ L.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If w ∉ L, may reject OR loop infinitely forever!<br/><br/>• <b>The Complement Duality Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>A language L is Recursive IF AND ONLY IF both L and its complement L' are Recursively Enumerable!</b>", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If L is RE and L' is also RE, then L is 100% DECIDABLE (Recursive)! If L is RE and undecidable, then L' CANNOT even be RE!"}, {'number': '22.2', 'title': "Rice's Theorem for Turing Machines", 'content': "• <b>Rice's Theorem (Part 1):</b> Any <b>non-trivial semantic (language) property</b> of the language accepted by a Turing Machine is <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>.<br/>• <b>What is a Semantic Property?</b> A question about the LANGUAGE recognized by the TM (e.g. 'Is L(M) empty?', 'Is L(M) finite?', 'Is L(M) regular?').<br/>• <b>Non-trivial means:</b> True for some TMs and False for other TMs.<br/>• <b>Syntactic Properties (Rice does NOT apply):</b> Questions about the machine structure itself (e.g. 'Does TM have 5 states?', 'Does TM take 10 steps on input w?') are DECIDABLE.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In GATE, if a question asks 'Is property P decidable for a Turing Machine?' and P is about L(M) (like regularity, emptiness, finiteness, equivalence), the answer is INSTANTLY UNDECIDABLE by Rice's Theorem!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Applying Rice's Theorem", 'problem': "Determine if the following problem is decidable: 'Given a Turing Machine M, does M accept a regular language?'", 'steps': ["Step 1: Check if the property is semantic: 'Is L(M) regular?' is a property of the language recognized by M (Semantic).", 'Step 2: Check if non-trivial: Some TMs accept regular languages, while others accept non-regular languages. Non-trivial!', "Step 3: By Rice's Theorem, the problem is UNDECIDABLE."], 'answer': "Undecidable by Rice's Theorem."}], 'exam_traps': ["'Does TM M take at most 100 steps on input w?' is DECIDABLE because you can simulate it for 100 steps and stop!", 'Post Correspondence Problem (PCP) is undecidable for alphabet size ≥ 2, but decidable for unary alphabet (size 1).']}, ('CD', 0): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Lexical Analysis, FIRST & FOLLOW Sets', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Spell Checker & Sentence Grammar Parser', 'text': "• Lexer: Reading characters and grouping them into valid vocabulary words ('int', 'sum', '100').\n• FIRST set: Asking 'what words could legally start a sentence?'.\n• FOLLOW set: Asking 'what word can legally appear immediately to the right of this word?'."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '23.1', 'title': 'Rules for Calculating FIRST Sets', 'content': '• <b>Definition:</b> FIRST(X) is the set of terminal symbols that appear as the first symbol of any string derived from X.<br/>• <b>The Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. If X is a terminal: <b>FIRST(X) = { X }</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If X → ε is a production: Add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If X → Y1 Y2 ... Yk:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y1) to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Y1 can derive ε, add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y2) to FIRST(X), and so forth.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If ALL Y1 ... Yk derive ε, add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Terminal symbols ALWAYS have FIRST(a) = { a }. When calculating FIRST(Y1 Y2), do not stop at Y1 if Y1 can produce ε!'}, {'number': '23.2', 'title': 'Rules for Calculating FOLLOW Sets', 'content': '• <b>Definition:</b> FOLLOW(A) is the set of terminals that can appear immediately to the right of non-terminal A.<br/>• <b>The Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. For Start Symbol S: Add <b>$ (End-of-input marker)</b> to FOLLOW(S).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. For production A → α B β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FIRST(β) except ε</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. For production A → α B OR A → α B β (where FIRST(β) contains ε):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FOLLOW(A)</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>• <b>FOLLOW sets NEVER contain ε!</b>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: FOLLOW set NEVER CONTAINS ε (epsilon)! If your calculation produces ε in a FOLLOW set, it is 100% WRONG!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Computing FIRST and FOLLOW Sets', 'problem': 'Given grammar: S → A B; A → a | ε; B → b | ε. Compute FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(A).', 'steps': ['Step 1: FIRST(A) = { a, ε }; FIRST(B) = { b, ε }.', 'Step 2: For S → A B: FIRST(S) includes FIRST(A) minus ε = { a }. Since A → ε, include FIRST(B) = { b }. Since both A and B can produce ε, add ε. ⇒ FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }.', 'Step 3: To find FOLLOW(A): Look at S → A B. Non-terminal B immediately follows A.', 'Step 4: Everything in FIRST(B) except ε goes into FOLLOW(A) ⇒ { b }.', 'Step 5: Since B derives ε, everything in FOLLOW(S) also goes into FOLLOW(A).', 'Step 6: S is start symbol ⇒ $ ∈ FOLLOW(S). Since S appears nowhere on RHS, FOLLOW(S) = { $ }.', 'Step 7: Therefore, FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.'], 'answer': 'FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }; FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In token counting: String constants like "Hello, World!" count as ONE single token!', "Identifiers vs Keywords: In C lexing, keywords like 'int', 'return' are reserved and not identifiers."]}, ('CD', 1): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Top-Down (LL(1)) & Bottom-Up (LR) Parsing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Predictive Keyboard & Assembling a Jigsaw Puzzle', 'text': '• LL(1) Parser: Like a predictive phone keyboard looking ahead by 1 letter to guess the next word without needing to delete and restart.\n• LR Parser: Assembling a jigsaw puzzle from individual bottom pieces upward until the entire picture is completed.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '24.1', 'title': 'LL(1) Grammar Conditions & Disqualifiers', 'content': '• A grammar is <b>LL(1)</b> if and only if for every production A → α | β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅</b> (disjoint FIRST sets).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. At most one of α or β can derive ε.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If β derives ε, then <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Instant Disqualifiers for LL(1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Any grammar with <b>Left Recursion (A → A α)</b> is NEVER LL(1)!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Any grammar with <b>Ambiguity</b> is NEVER LL(1)!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Left recursion (immediate or indirect) INSTANTLY disqualifies a grammar from being LL(1)! Eliminate left recursion and left factoring before building LL(1) table.'}, {'number': '24.2', 'title': 'Hierarchy and Power of LR Parsers', 'content': '• <b>Power Hierarchy (Strict Containment):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ CLR(1)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of Parsing States:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>States in LR(0) == States in SLR(1) == States in LALR(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- CLR(1) has significantly MORE states (splits states with different lookaheads).<br/><br/>• <b>Conflict Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Merging CLR(1) states to form LALR(1) <b>NEVER produces Shift-Reduce (SR) conflicts</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- It can ONLY introduce Reduce-Reduce (RR) conflicts.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Merging CLR(1) states into LALR(1) can NEVER introduce a new Shift-Reduce conflict! It can only introduce Reduce-Reduce conflicts.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Checking if Grammar is LL(1)', 'problem': 'Is grammar S → a S | a LL(1)?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Check productions for S: α = a S and β = a.', 'Step 2: FIRST(α) = { a }; FIRST(β) = { a }.', 'Step 3: Check intersection: FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = { a } ≠ ∅.', "Step 4: Since intersection is not empty, the parser cannot decide which rule to choose when lookahead is 'a'."], 'answer': "Grammar is NOT LL(1) (requires left factoring to S → a S' with S' → S | ε)."}], 'exam_traps': ['Every SLR(1) grammar is an LALR(1) grammar, but not vice versa!', 'Every ambiguous grammar is neither LL(1) nor LR(k) for any k.']}, ('CD', 2): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'SDT, Intermediate Code & Optimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Inheritance Tree & Unused Clutter Removal', 'text': '• S-attributed: Like kids calculating their allowance and reporting it UP to their parents (Bottom-Up).\n• L-attributed: Like parents distributing inheritance to children from left to right.\n• Dead Code Elimination: Throwing away broken, unused furniture from an attic so you never waste time cleaning it.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '25.1', 'title': 'S-Attributed vs L-Attributed SDT', 'content': '• <b>S-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses ONLY <b>Synthesized Attributes</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Evaluated strictly bottom-up (postorder traversal).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Naturally evaluated during <b>LR parsing (bottom-up)</b> without modifying parse stack.<br/><br/>• <b>L-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses Synthesized attributes AND restricted Inherited attributes.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- An inherited attribute can depend only on:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Attributes of symbols to its <b>LEFT</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Inherited attributes of its <b>parent</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Evaluated during <b>Depth-First Search (left-to-right)</b> traversal.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every S-attributed SDT is automatically an L-attributed SDT! The reverse is NOT true: L-attributed includes inherited attributes that depend on left siblings.'}, {'number': '25.2', 'title': 'Common Code Optimizations & Basic Blocks', 'content': '• <b>Leaders in 3-Address Code:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Statement 1 is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Target of any jump (GOTO) is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Statement immediately following any jump is a leader.<br/><br/>• <b>Optimization Types:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Folding:</b> Compute constant operations at compile-time: <code>x = 2 * 3.14</code> becomes <code>x = 6.28</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Propagation:</b> Replace variable with its known constant value.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dead Code Elimination:</b> Remove code whose result is never used.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strength Reduction:</b> Replace expensive operations with cheaper ones: <code>x * 2</code> replaced by <code>x << 1</code> or <code>x + x</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strength reduction replaces an expensive operation with a cheaper one (e.g. multiplication replaced by addition in loops). It does NOT eliminate code!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Identifying Leaders and Basic Blocks', 'problem': 'Given: (1) i = 1; (2) j = 1; (3) t1 = 10 * i; (4) t2 = t1 + j; (5) if t2 < 100 goto (3); (6) goto (1). How many basic blocks?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Statement (1) is a leader (first statement).', 'Step 2: Target of goto (3) is statement (3) ⇒ (3) is a leader.', 'Step 3: Target of goto (1) is statement (1) (already a leader).', 'Step 4: Statement following jump (5) is statement (6) ⇒ (6) is a leader.', 'Step 5: Leaders are (1), (3), and (6).', 'Step 6: Block 1: [(1), (2)]; Block 2: [(3), (4), (5)]; Block 3: [(6)].'], 'answer': 'Total Basic Blocks = 3.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In L-attributed SDT, actions can be placed anywhere, but they can only use values from elements to their LEFT!', 'Three-address code has at most 3 addresses per instruction (e.g. x = y op z).']}, ('OS', 0): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Process Management, CPU Scheduling & Threads', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Cooking on a 1-Burner Stove', 'text': '• CPU Scheduling: Like a chef with 1 stove burner (CPU) cooking 4 dishes (processes). FCFS finishes dish 1 completely before starting dish 2. Round Robin cooks each dish for 2 minutes, switching rapidly so no customer starves!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '26.1', 'title': 'The fork() System Call & Process Tree', 'content': "• <code>fork()</code> clones the calling process to create an exact child process.<br/>• <b>Return Value of fork():</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>0 to the Child process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>Child's PID (> 0) to the Parent process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>negative</b> on failure.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of Processes Created:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>fork()</code> is executed <b>n times</b> sequentially: Total processes = <b>2^n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of <b>Child processes</b> created = <b>2^n - 1</b>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In short-circuit expressions like fork() && fork(): the second fork executes ONLY in the parent process because fork() returns 0 (False) to the child, short-circuiting the &&!'}, {'number': '26.2', 'title': 'CPU Scheduling Metrics & Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Turnaround Time (TAT):</b> Completion Time - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Waiting Time (WT):</b> Turnaround Time - Burst Time.<br/>• <b>Response Time:</b> First Time CPU is Allocated - Arrival Time.<br/><br/>• <b>Algorithm Highlights:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>SJF / SRTF:</b> Provably gives <b>minimum Average Waiting Time</b> among all scheduling algorithms!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Round Robin (RR):</b> Time quantum q. If q is very large, RR becomes FCFS; if q is tiny, context switch overhead dominates.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Convoy Effect:</b> Occurs in FCFS when a long CPU-bound process blocks short I/O-bound processes.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SRTF (Shortest Remaining Time First) is preemptive SJF and gives minimum average waiting time, but can cause starvation for long processes!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating Number of Created Processes', 'problem': 'How many total child processes are created by: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { fork(); } ?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Loop runs 3 times, executing fork() 3 times unconditionally.', 'Step 2: Total processes = 2^n where n = 3 ⇒ 2^3 = 8 total processes.', 'Step 3: Total child processes = 2^n - 1 = 8 - 1 = 7 child processes.'], 'answer': 'Total child processes created = 7.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Do not confuse total processes (2^n) with total child processes (2^n - 1)!', 'Non-preemptive algorithms NEVER interrupt a process once it is running on the CPU.']}, ('OS', 1): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Process Synchronization, Semaphores & Deadlock Prevention', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Single-Key Restroom & 4-Way Traffic Gridlock', 'text': '• Semaphore: A single restroom key at a gas station. Only the person holding the key can enter (Mutual Exclusion). Others must wait until the key is returned (Wait/Signal).\n• Deadlock: A 4-way intersection gridlock where every car is blocked by the car in front of it, and no one can move forward!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '27.1', 'title': 'Critical Section Requirements & Counting Semaphores', 'content': '• <b>The 3 Mandatory Requirements for Critical Section (CS):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion (Mandatory):</b> If Pi is in CS, no other process can enter CS.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Progress (Mandatory):</b> Only processes competing to enter CS decide who enters next.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Bounded Waiting:</b> A bound must exist on entries by others before a waiting process gets its turn.<br/><br/>• <b>Counting Semaphores Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Initialized to S0 (available resources).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Final Semaphore Value = S0 - (Wait operations) + (Signal operations)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If final value is negative (-k): <b>Exactly k processes are blocked in the waiting queue!</b>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If counting semaphore is initialized to S0, after p wait operations and v signal operations, final value = S0 - p + v. If final value is negative (-k), exactly k processes are blocked in the waiting queue!'}, {'number': '27.2', 'title': "Deadlock: 4 Necessary Conditions & Banker's Algorithm", 'content': "• <b>The 4 Coffman Conditions (Must ALL hold for deadlock):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Mutual Exclusion<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Hold and Wait<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. No Preemption<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Circular Wait<br/><br/>• <b>Banker's Algorithm:</b> Deadlock avoidance via Safe State analysis.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Need Matrix = Max Requirement - Current Allocation</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If available resources can satisfy Need of a process, it finishes and returns its allocation to Available.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An unsafe state does NOT mean deadlock! Every deadlocked state is unsafe, but an unsafe state may or may not lead to deadlock.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Counting Semaphore Value and Blocked Processes', 'problem': 'A counting semaphore S is initialized to 10. Then 14 wait() operations and 3 signal() operations are executed on S. What is the final value of S, and how many processes are blocked?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula: Final S = Initial + Signals - Waits.', 'Step 2: Final S = 10 + 3 - 14 = 13 - 14 = -1.', 'Step 3: When S is negative, the magnitude |S| represents the number of blocked processes.', 'Step 4: | -1 | = 1 process is blocked.'], 'answer': 'Final value of S = -1; Exactly 1 process is blocked.'}], 'exam_traps': ["Peterson's algorithm works strictly for TWO processes, not general n processes without modifications!", 'Strict alternation (using only turn variable) satisfies Mutual Exclusion, but FAILS Progress.']}, ('OS', 2): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Paging, Virtual Memory, TLB & Inodes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Novel Chapter Index & Speed Dial', 'text': "• Paging: Dividing a 500-page book into 10-page chapters so only the chapter you are currently reading needs to be open on your desk.\n• TLB: A smartphone speed-dial list storing your top 8 contacts so you don't look up their numbers in the full directory each time."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '28.1', 'title': 'Paging & Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with TLB', 'content': '• <b>Virtual Address Split: [ Page Number (p) | Page Offset (d) ]</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Offset = <code>log2(Page Size)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Number = Virtual Address Bits - Page Offset.<br/>• <b>Page Table Size:</b> <code>(Number of Pages) × (Page Table Entry PTE Size)</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with 1-level Paging:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>EMAT = Hit_Rate × (t_{tlb} + t_m) + (1 - Hit_Rate) × (t_{tlb} + 2 × t_m)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;On TLB Hit: 1 memory access (for data word).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;On TLB Miss: 2 memory accesses (1 for Page Table Entry + 1 for data word)!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a TLB hit, memory is accessed ONCE (for data). On a TLB miss in 1-level paging, memory is accessed TWICE (first for page table entry, second for data word)!'}, {'number': '28.2', 'title': "Page Replacement Algorithms & Belady's Anomaly", 'content': "• <b>FIFO (First-In First-Out):</b> Replaces the oldest loaded page.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Suffers from <b>Belady's Anomaly</b>: Increasing the number of page frames can INCREASE the number of page faults!<br/>• <b>LRU (Least Recently Used):</b> Replaces page not used for longest time in the past. (Stack algorithm; NEVER suffers from Belady's Anomaly).<br/>• <b>Optimal Page Replacement (OPT / MIN):</b> Replaces page that will not be used for longest time in the FUTURE. (Provably minimum page faults).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Belady's Anomaly occurs in FIFO! Stack algorithms like LRU and Optimal NEVER suffer from Belady's Anomaly."}, {'number': '28.3', 'title': 'Unix Inodes & Maximum File Size', 'content': '• <b>Disk Block Pointer Hierarchy in an Inode:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Direct Pointers (e.g. 12 pointers):</b> 12 × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Single Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size) × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Double Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)² × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Triple Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)³ × Block Size.<br/>• <b>Max File Size = Sum of all addressable bytes across direct, single, double, and triple pointers!</b>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Number of pointers per indirect block = Block Size / Disk Pointer Size. For 1 KB blocks and 4-byte pointers: 1024 / 4 = 256 pointers per block!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: EMAT Calculation with TLB', 'problem': 'A system has TLB access time of 20 ns and main memory access time of 100 ns. The TLB hit ratio is 90%. What is the effective memory access time EMAT?', 'steps': ['Step 1: On TLB hit: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 120 ns.', 'Step 2: On TLB miss: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for Page Table (100 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 220 ns.', 'Step 3: EMAT = (0.90 × 120 ns) + (0.10 × 220 ns).', 'Step 4: EMAT = 108 ns + 22 ns = 130 ns.'], 'answer': 'EMAT = 130 ns.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In multi-level paging with k levels: On TLB miss, you need k memory accesses for page tables + 1 memory access for data = (k + 1) memory accesses!', 'Internal fragmentation occurs on the last page of a process; paging completely eliminates external fragmentation.']}, ('DB', 0): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'ER-Model, Relational Algebra & SQL', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: University Enrollment & Spreadsheet Database', 'text': '• ER Model: The architectural blueprint of a university database showing Students, Courses, and Departments.\n• Relational Algebra: Filter rows (Select σ), pick specific columns (Project π), and merge tables on common keys (Natural Join ⨝).'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '29.1', 'title': 'ER to Relational Table Conversion Rules (Minimal Tables)', 'content': "• <b>Converting Relationships into Minimal Tables:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Relationship Cardinality   Table Conversion Rule                                  Min Tables<br/>---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Many-to-Many (M:N)         Requires a separate relationship table (FKs of both)   3 tables<br/>One-to-Many (1:N)          Combine into 'Many' side table using '1' side PK as FK 2 tables<br/>One-to-One (1:1)           Can merge into either table (or 1 table if total part) 2 (or 1)</div>• <b>Weak Entity:</b> Has no primary key of its own; uses discriminator and owner entity's primary key.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Weak entity and its identifying relationship are ALWAYS combined into <b>1 single table</b>.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In an M:N relationship, you ALWAYS need a separate third relationship table! In a 1:N relationship, never create a separate relationship table—place the primary key of the '1' side as a foreign key on the 'N' side!"}, {'number': '29.2', 'title': 'Relational Algebra vs SQL Rules & NULL Logic', 'content': '• <b>Relational Algebra:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selection (σ):</b> Filters rows matching predicate.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Projection (π):</b> Selects columns and <b>AUTOMATICALLY ELIMINATES DUPLICATES</b>!<br/>• <b>SQL Duplicate Handling:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;SQL preserves duplicate rows by default! You must explicitly write <code>SELECT DISTINCT</code> to remove duplicates.<br/><br/>• <b>Three-Valued Logic in SQL (TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Comparisons with <code>NULL</code> (like <code>x = NULL</code> or <code>NULL = NULL</code>) evaluate to <b>UNKNOWN</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Rows where WHERE condition evaluates to UNKNOWN or FALSE are NOT returned.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: π in Relational Algebra eliminates duplicates automatically, but SELECT in SQL preserves duplicates unless DISTINCT is explicitly written! COUNT(*) counts rows including NULLs; COUNT(col) ignores NULLs.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Minimal Tables for ER Schema', 'problem': 'Entities E1, E2, E3. Relationship R1 between E1 and E2 is M:N. Relationship R2 between E2 and E3 is 1:N. How many minimal tables are required?', 'steps': ['Step 1: E1 table is created.', 'Step 2: E2 table is created.', 'Step 3: M:N relationship R1 requires a separate relationship table (R1_table).', 'Step 4: 1:N relationship R2: E3 can store FK of E2 ⇒ No separate table for R2.', 'Step 5: E3 table is created (with FK referencing E2).', 'Step 6: Total tables: E1, E2, R1_table, E3 = 4 tables.'], 'answer': 'Minimum number of tables = 4.'}], 'exam_traps': ["In SQL, WHERE NULL = NULL returns 0 rows! To check for nulls, you must write 'WHERE col IS NULL'.", 'HAVING clause filters groups AFTER aggregation; WHERE clause filters individual rows BEFORE grouping.']}, ('DB', 1): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Functional Dependencies, Normalization & B+ Trees', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Student Roll Number & Dictionary Thumb Index', 'text': "• Functional Dependency: Like a student roll number uniquely determining their name, email, and branch (RollNo -> Name, Email, Branch).\n• B+ Tree: The alphabetical thumb-tabs on a 2000-page physical dictionary, letting you jump to 'Network' in 3 flips instead of flipping through every single page!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '30.1', 'title': 'The Step-by-Step Normal Forms Checklist (1NF to BCNF)', 'content': '• <b>1NF:</b> Every attribute contains strictly <b>atomic values</b> (no sets or lists).<br/>• <b>2NF:</b> Relation is in 1NF AND contains <b>NO Partial Dependency</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;(No non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of any candidate key).<br/>• <b>3NF:</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Either <b>X is a Superkey</b>,<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• OR <b>Y is a Prime Attribute</b> (part of some candidate key).<br/>• <b>BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form):</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>X MUST BE A SUPERKEY!</b>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 3NF, the RHS (Y) is allowed to be a prime attribute even if LHS (X) is not a superkey! In BCNF, this exception is REMOVED: LHS (X) MUST be a superkey!'}, {'number': '30.2', 'title': 'Decomposition Guarantees: Lossless Join & Dependency Preservation', 'content': '• <b>Lossless Join Test (for binary decomposition R1, R2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Decomposition is Lossless IF AND ONLY IF common attribute is a superkey of at least one relation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>(R1 ∩ R2) → R1 &nbsp;OR&nbsp; (R1 ∩ R2) → R2</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Decomposition Guarantees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into 3NF</b> that is BOTH Lossless AND Dependency Preserving.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into BCNF</b> that is Lossless, but Dependency Preservation is NOT guaranteed!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: BCNF decomposition guarantees Lossless Join, but may LOSE functional dependencies! 3NF decomposition guarantees BOTH Lossless Join AND Dependency Preservation.'}, {'number': '30.3', 'title': 'B-Tree vs B+ Tree Node Structure & Search Order', 'content': '• <b>B-Tree:</b> Keys and record pointers are stored in BOTH internal and leaf nodes.<br/>• <b>B+ Tree:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Data pointers are stored <b>ONLY in Leaf Nodes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Leaf nodes are connected as a <b>linked list</b> for fast sequential range scans.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal nodes contain only routing keys and child pointers (higher fanout!).<br/>• <b>Node Capacities (Order p):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal node holds at most <b>p pointers</b> and <b>p - 1 keys</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum keys in internal node: <b>⌈p / 2⌉ - 1</b> (except root: min 1 key).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In B+ Trees, internal nodes only store routing keys (no data pointers)! Hence internal nodes fit far more keys per disk block than standard B-Trees, keeping tree height very small (typically 3 to 4).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Finding Highest Normal Form', 'problem': 'Relation R(A, B, C, D) with FDs: { AB → C, C → D, D → A }. What is the highest normal form of R?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Compute candidate keys: (AB)+ = {A,B,C,D} ⇒ AB is a key. (BC)+ = {B,C,D,A} ⇒ BC is a key. (BD)+ = {B,D,A,C} ⇒ BD is a key.', 'Step 2: Candidate keys: AB, BC, BD. Prime attributes = { A, B, C, D } (ALL attributes are prime!).', 'Step 3: Check 2NF: No non-prime attributes exist ⇒ 2NF holds trivially!', 'Step 4: Check 3NF: For C → D, D is prime! For D → A, A is prime! Since RHS is always prime, 3NF holds!', 'Step 5: Check BCNF: In C → D, C is not a superkey. Fails BCNF!'], 'answer': 'Highest normal form is 3NF.'}], 'exam_traps': ['If ALL attributes in a relation are Prime Attributes, the relation is GUARANTEED to be in 3NF!', 'Order of B-tree p means maximum child pointers; maximum keys is always p - 1.']}, ('DB', 2): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Transactions, Serializability & Two-Phase Locking (2PL)', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: ATM Cash Withdrawal & Bank Balance Consistency', 'text': "• Atomicity: If an ATM dispenses $100 cash but the machine loses power before updating your account balance, the system automatically rolls back so your money isn't lost.\n• Conflict Serializability: Making concurrent transactions execute as if they ran one after another in some clean serial order."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '31.1', 'title': 'Conflict Serializability & Precedence Graphs', 'content': '• <b>Two operations are in CONFLICT if:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. They belong to <b>different transactions</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. They access the <b>same data item</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>At least one of them is a WRITE</b> operation.<br/><br/>• <b>Precedence Graph (Testing Conflict Serializability):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Draw directed edge <b>Ti → Tj</b> if Ti executes a conflicting operation BEFORE Tj.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Testing Rule:</b> Schedule is Conflict Serializable <b>IF AND ONLY IF the precedence graph has NO CYCLES (Acyclic)!</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Equivalent serial order = <b>Topological Sort</b> of the graph.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If precedence graph contains a cycle (e.g. T1 → T2 → T1), it is 100% NOT conflict serializable! A schedule without cycles is always conflict serializable.'}, {'number': '31.2', 'title': 'Two-Phase Locking (2PL) Protocol', 'content': '• <b>Basic 2PL:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Growing Phase:</b> May acquire locks, cannot release any lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Shrinking Phase:</b> May release locks, cannot acquire any new lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Guarantees Conflict Serializability!</b> But DOES NOT prevent deadlocks or cascading aborts.<br/><br/>• <b>Strict 2PL:</b> Holds all EXCLUSIVE (X) write locks until Commit/Abort. (Prevents cascading rollbacks!).<br/>• <b>Rigorous 2PL:</b> Holds ALL locks (shared and exclusive) until Commit/Abort.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Basic 2PL guarantees Conflict Serializability, but is NOT free from Deadlocks! Strict 2PL is Recoverable and Cascading-Free.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Conflict Serializability Cycle Detection', 'problem': 'Given schedule S: r1(X), r2(Y), w1(Y), w2(X), c1, c2. Is S conflict serializable?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Conflict on X: r1(X) then w2(X) ⇒ T1 executes before T2 ⇒ Edge T1 → T2.', 'Step 2: Conflict on Y: r2(Y) then w1(Y) ⇒ T2 executes before T1 ⇒ Edge T2 → T1.', 'Step 3: Graph contains directed cycle T1 ⇄ T2.', 'Step 4: Since precedence graph has a cycle, S is NOT conflict serializable.'], 'answer': 'Schedule S is NOT conflict serializable.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Every conflict serializable schedule is view serializable, but not vice versa! View serializability allows blind writes.', 'ACID: Durability guarantees that committed changes survive power failures using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL).']}, ('CN', 0): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'OSI/TCP-IP Layers, Framing & CRC', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Postal Mail Envelope & Barcode Checksum', 'text': '• Layered Architecture: Writing a letter (Application), translating it to Spanish (Presentation), putting it in an envelope (Transport), writing address (Network), placing in delivery truck (Data Link), sending along highway (Physical).\n• CRC: The checksum barcode on the back of an envelope that detects if a digit got smudged in the rain.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '32.1', 'title': 'CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) Polynomial Division', 'content': '• Let generator polynomial G(x) have degree <b>k</b> (i.e. k + 1 bits).<br/>• <b>Encoding Steps:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Append <b>k zeros</b> to the end of the original data frame.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Perform <b>Modulo-2 Binary Division</b> (using XOR instead of subtraction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. The k-bit remainder is the <b>CRC Checksum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Transmitted frame = <b>Original Data + CRC Remainder</b>.<br/>• <b>Receiver Check:</b> Receiver divides received frame by G(x). If remainder is all 0s, frame is accepted.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If generator polynomial has degree k, append exactly k zeros to data! Modulo-2 division uses XOR (0⊕0=0, 1⊕1=0, 0⊕1=1, 1⊕0=1). Never use arithmetic borrowing!'}, {'number': '32.2', 'title': 'Hamming Code & Error Correction', 'content': '• <b>Parity Bits Formula:</b> For m data bits and p parity bits:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>2^p ≥ m + p + 1</b>.<br/>• <b>Hamming Distance (d):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To detect up to <b>d errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>d + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To correct up to <b>t errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>2t + 1</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To correct t single-bit errors, minimum distance d_min ≥ 2t + 1! To detect t errors, d_min ≥ t + 1.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating CRC Transmitted Frame', 'problem': 'Given Data = 100100 and Generator polynomial G(x) = x³ + x² + 1 (binary 1101). Find the CRC remainder and transmitted bit sequence.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Degree of G(x) is 3 ⇒ Append 3 zeros to data: 100100 000.', 'Step 2: Divide 100100000 by 1101 using Modulo-2 XOR division.', 'Step 3: 3-bit remainder is 001.', 'Step 4: Transmitted codeword = 100100 001.'], 'answer': 'CRC remainder = 001; Transmitted frame = 100100001.'}], 'exam_traps': ["In bit stuffing: Sender inserts a '0' after FIVE consecutive 1s! Flag is 01111110 (six 1s).", 'Physical layer deals with bits, Data Link with frames, Network with packets, Transport with segments.']}, ('CN', 1): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Flow Control Protocols & CSMA/CD', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Walkie-Talkie & Crowded Dinner Party', 'text': "• Stop-and-Wait: Like a walkie-talkie conversation. You say 'Over' and wait until the other person acknowledges before speaking again.\n• CSMA/CD: Like people at a dinner party. You listen before speaking (Carrier Sense). If two speak at once (Collision), both stop, wait a random time (Backoff), and try again."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '33.1', 'title': 'Sliding Window Protocol Efficiency Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Key Parameters:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Transmission Time: <code>Tt = Frame Size (L) / Bandwidth (B)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Propagation Time: <code>Tp = Distance (d) / Velocity (v)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parameter a: <code>a = Tp / Tt</code>.<br/>• <b>Efficiency Formulas:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Stop-and-Wait:</b> <code>η = 1 / (1 + 2a)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Sliding Window with window size W:</b> <code>η = W / (1 + 2a)</code> (capped at 100%).<br/><br/>• <b>Window Sizes for k-bit sequence numbers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Go-Back-N (GBN):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^k - 1</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selective Repeat (SR):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Selective Repeat (SR), Ws + Wr ≤ 2^k. Since Ws = Wr, maximum window size is 2^(k-1)! In GBN, Ws ≤ 2^k - 1 and Wr = 1.'}, {'number': '33.2', 'title': 'CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Size', 'content': '• <b>Collision Detection Condition:</b> Transmission time MUST be at least twice propagation time:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Tt ≥ 2 × Tp</b>.<br/>• <b>Minimum Frame Size Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>L_min ≥ 2 × Tp × Bandwidth</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;= <b>2 × (Distance / Velocity) × Bandwidth</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In CSMA/CD, L_min = 2 × Tp × Bandwidth! If frame is smaller than L_min, sender finishes transmitting before collision signal arrives, failing to detect collision.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Length', 'problem': 'A 1 km network has bandwidth 1 Gbps and signal velocity 2 × 10^8 m/s. Calculate the minimum frame size for CSMA/CD.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Distance d = 1000 m; Velocity v = 2 × 10^8 m/s.', 'Step 2: Tp = d / v = 1000 / (2 × 10^8) = 5 × 10^(-6) seconds = 5 µs.', 'Step 3: Bandwidth B = 1 Gbps = 10^9 bits/sec.', 'Step 4: L_min = 2 × Tp × B = 2 × (5 × 10^(-6)) × 10^9 = 10 × 10^3 = 10,000 bits = 1250 bytes.'], 'answer': 'Minimum frame size = 10,000 bits (1250 bytes).'}], 'exam_traps': ['In GBN, out-of-order packets are silently discarded because receiver window size is strictly 1!', 'In Selective Repeat, independent acknowledgments are sent for each packet, requiring memory buffers at receiver.']}, ('CN', 2): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'IPv4, Subnetting, Routing & Fragmentation', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Slicing a Pizza & Oversized Cargo Disassembly', 'text': '• Subnetting: Slicing a giant pizza into smaller slices so each office department gets exactly what they need without wasting slices.\n• Fragmentation: Shipping a sofa that cannot fit through the front door (MTU), so you take it apart into 3 pieces and reassemble inside!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '34.1', 'title': 'IPv4 Subnetting & Usable Hosts', 'content': '• In CIDR notation <b>a.b.c.d / n</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Network bits = <b>n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Host bits = <b>32 - n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Total IP addresses = <b>2^(32 - n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Usable Host addresses = 2^(32 - n) - 2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Subtract 2: First address is Network ID; Last address is Directed Broadcast Address).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a /30 subnet: 2^(32-30) = 4 IPs. Minus 2 = exactly 2 usable host addresses (ideal for point-to-point router links)!'}, {'number': '34.2', 'title': 'Fragmentation Offset Calculation Rules', 'content': "• <b>Key Fragmentation Header Fields:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>MF (More Fragments) Flag:</b> 1 for all fragments EXCEPT the last fragment (MF = 0 for last).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DF (Don't Fragment) Flag:</b> If 1 and packet exceeds MTU, packet is dropped.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Fragment Offset:</b> Offset field in IPv4 header is measured in units of <b>8 bytes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Offset = (Byte offset of data) / 8</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Data payload in each fragment (except last) MUST be a multiple of 8 bytes!</b>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fragment offset is measured in units of 8 BYTES! Subtract the 20-byte IP header first: if MTU = 1500 bytes, max data payload is 1480 bytes (1480 / 8 = 185, a valid multiple of 8)!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: IPv4 Packet Fragmentation', 'problem': 'An IP datagram of 4000 bytes (including 20-byte IP header) arrives at a router where MTU = 1500 bytes. Find payload size, MF flag, and offset for each fragment.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Total data = 4000 - 20 = 3980 bytes.', 'Step 2: Max payload per fragment = 1500 - 20 = 1480 bytes (multiple of 8).', 'Step 3: Frag 1: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 0 / 8 = 0, MF = 1.', 'Step 4: Frag 2: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 1480 / 8 = 185, MF = 1.', 'Step 5: Remaining data = 3980 - 2960 = 1020 bytes.', 'Step 6: Frag 3: Payload = 1020 bytes, Offset = 2960 / 8 = 370, MF = 0.'], 'answer': 'Frag 1: 1480B, MF=1, Off=0; Frag 2: 1480B, MF=1, Off=185; Frag 3: 1020B, MF=0, Off=370.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Reassembly of fragments is performed ONLY at the DESTINATION host, never at intermediate routers!', 'Distance Vector routing suffers from Count-to-Infinity problem; solved by Split Horizon and Poison Reverse.']}, ('CN', 3): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'TCP Congestion Control & Application Protocols', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Highway Speed Limit & Highway Traffic Jam', 'text': '• TCP Slow Start: Starting your car gently and doubling speed every block until you reach the threshold speed limit.\n• Congestion Avoidance: Once past the threshold, increasing speed by only 1 km/h each block to avoid crashing into traffic ahead!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '35.1', 'title': 'TCP Congestion Window Phases', 'content': '• <b>1. Slow Start Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Congestion Window (cwnd) starts at 1 MSS (Maximum Segment Size).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For every ACK received, cwnd increases by 1 MSS (effectively <b>DOUBLES every RTT</b>: exponential growth).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Continues until cwnd reaches <b>ssthresh (slow-start threshold)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>2. Congestion Avoidance Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- cwnd increases by <b>1 MSS per RTT</b> (additive increase: linear growth).<br/><br/>• <b>3. On Loss Event:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Timeout (Severe Loss):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd resets to 1 MSS</b> (re-enters Slow Start).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>3 Duplicate ACKs (Mild Loss - Fast Retransmit):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd = ssthresh</b> (Fast Recovery).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On 3 duplicate ACKs, cwnd drops to ssthresh (not 1 MSS!) and enters Congestion Avoidance. On Timeout, cwnd resets all the way down to 1 MSS!'}, {'number': '35.2', 'title': 'Application Layer Protocols Summary', 'content': '• <b>DNS (Port 53):</b> Uses <b>UDP</b> for standard queries (fast); uses <b>TCP</b> for zone transfers.<br/>• <b>HTTP (Port 80) / HTTPS (Port 443):</b> Uses <b>TCP</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Non-persistent HTTP/1.0: 2 RTTs per object.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Persistent HTTP/1.1: 1 RTT per object after initial connection handshake.<br/>• <b>Email Protocols:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>SMTP (Port 25):</b> Push protocol (client to mail server, server to server).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>POP3 (Port 110) / IMAP (Port 143):</b> Pull protocols (client downloading mail from server).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SMTP is a PUSH protocol! POP3 and IMAP are PULL protocols! DNS uses UDP for queries but TCP for zone transfers.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Tracing TCP cwnd Growth', 'problem': 'A TCP connection has ssthresh = 16 KB and MSS = 2 KB. Starting from cwnd = 2 KB, trace cwnd after 5 RTTs (no loss).', 'steps': ['Step 1: Round 0: cwnd = 2 KB.', 'Step 2: RTT 1 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 4 KB.', 'Step 3: RTT 2 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 8 KB.', 'Step 4: RTT 3 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 16 KB (reaches ssthresh).', 'Step 5: RTT 4 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases linearly by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 18 KB.', 'Step 6: RTT 5 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 20 KB.'], 'answer': 'After 5 RTTs, cwnd = 20 KB.'}], 'exam_traps': ['TCP uses byte sequence numbers, NOT packet sequence numbers! If packet carries 100 bytes starting at seq 500, next packet has seq 600.', 'Silently discarding packets by UDP provides zero flow or congestion control.']}, ('GA', 0): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Quantitative Aptitude - Work, Speed, Ratios & Percentages', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Two Taps Filling a Bathtub', 'text': '• Work and Time: Tap A fills a tub in 10 minutes (rate = 1/10 per min); Tap B fills it in 15 minutes (rate = 1/15 per min). Working together, their speeds add up: 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6, filling the tub in 6 minutes!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '36.1', 'title': 'Time, Speed, Distance & Relative Speed', 'content': '• <b>Fundamental Formula:</b> <b>Distance = Speed × Time</b>.<br/>• <b>Unit Conversion:</b> <b>1 km/h = 5 / 18 m/s</b> &nbsp;and&nbsp; 1 m/s = 18 / 5 km/h.<br/>• <b>Average Speed:</b> <b>Total Distance / Total Time</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If equal distances covered at speeds v1 and v2: <b>Average Speed = 2 · v1 · v2 / (v1 + v2)</b> (Harmonic Mean!).<br/>• <b>Relative Speed:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Opposite directions:</b> Relative Speed = <b>v1 + v2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Same direction:</b> Relative Speed = <b>|v1 - v2|</b>.<br/>• <b>Boats and Streams:</b> Downstream speed = <b>u + v</b>; Upstream speed = <b>u - v</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Average speed is NEVER the simple arithmetic mean (v1 + v2)/2! For equal distance intervals, average speed is always 2·v1·v2 / (v1 + v2).'}, {'number': '36.2', 'title': 'Time & Work (The LCM Efficiency Method)', 'content': '• <b>LCM Method:</b> Assume Total Work = <b>LCM of all individual completion times</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Calculate 1-day efficiency = Total Work / Individual Days.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Combined efficiency = Sum of individual efficiencies.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Total Days = Total Work / Combined Efficiency.<br/>• <b>Pipes and Cisterns:</b> Inlet pipe adds positive work (+); Outlet pipe subtracts negative work (-).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Using the LCM method eliminates messy fractions completely! If A takes 12 days and B takes 18 days: Total Work = LCM(12, 18) = 36 units. Efficiency of A = 3, B = 2. Together = 36 / 5 = 7.2 days!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Two Trains Crossing Each Other', 'problem': 'Two trains of lengths 120 m and 180 m travel in opposite directions at 40 km/h and 50 km/h. How many seconds do they take to pass each other?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Total distance = Sum of train lengths = 120 + 180 = 300 meters.', 'Step 2: Relative speed in opposite directions = 40 + 50 = 90 km/h.', 'Step 3: Convert speed to m/s: 90 × (5 / 18) = 25 m/s.', 'Step 4: Time taken = Distance / Speed = 300 m / (25 m/s) = 12 seconds.'], 'answer': 'Time taken = 12 seconds.'}], 'exam_traps': ['When a train crosses a pole or person, distance covered = length of train alone (pole has negligible width).', 'Successive percentage discount of x% and y% is NOT (x + y)%! Equivalent discount = x + y - (xy / 100)%.']}, ('GA', 1): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Analytical & Spatial Reasoning - Blood Relations, Clocks & Cubes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Analogy: Rubik's Cube Slicing & Wall Clock Hands", 'text': "• Painted Cube: Like a 3x3x3 Rubik's cube painted red on outside. Only the 8 corner pieces have 3 painted faces! Center pieces inside the cube have 0 painted faces.\n• Clock Hands: At 12:00 both hands point straight up. By 12:30, the hour hand has already crept halfway toward 1 (15 degrees)!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '37.1', 'title': 'Clock Angle Formula', 'content': '• Angle between Hour and Minute hand at H hours and M minutes:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Angle θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |</b>.<br/>• <b>Speeds of Hands:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Minute hand speed: <b>6° per minute</b> (360° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Hour hand speed: <b>0.5° per minute</b> (30° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Relative speed: <b>5.5° per minute</b>.<br/>• <b>Coincidence (0°):</b> Hands coincide <b>22 times in 24 hours</b> (not 24 times, due to 11-12-1 overlap).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Use θ = |30H - 5.5M|! If angle is greater than 180°, reflex angle is 360° - θ.'}, {'number': '37.2', 'title': 'Painted Cube Slicing Formulas', 'content': '• A large cube painted on all outside faces is cut into <b>n³ smaller equal cubes</b> (n cuts per edge):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>3 Faces Painted (Corners):</b> ALWAYS exactly <b>8 cubes</b> (for any n ≥ 2).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>2 Faces Painted (Edges):</b> <b>12 × (n - 2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>1 Face Painted (Face Centers):</b> <b>6 × (n - 2)²</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>0 Faces Painted (Inner Core):</b> <b>(n - 2)³</b>.<br/>• <b>Verification:</b> 8 + 12(n - 2) + 6(n - 2)² + (n - 2)³ = n³.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regardless of the value of n, cubes with 3 faces painted is ALWAYS 8 (the 8 corners of the cube)!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Angle between Clock Hands', 'problem': 'Find the angle between the hour hand and minute hand of a clock at 3:40.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Here H = 3, M = 40.', 'Step 2: Formula: θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |.', 'Step 3: 30 × 3 = 90; (11 / 2) × 40 = 220.', 'Step 4: θ = | 90 - 220 | = | -130 | = 130°.'], 'answer': 'Angle between hands = 130°.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In calendar problems, remember that century years like 1900 are NOT leap years unless divisible by 400 (2000 is a leap year)!', "In blood relations, never assume gender from a person's name unless explicitly stated or implied by relationship."]}, ('GA', 2): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Verbal Aptitude - Grammar, Concord & Critical Reasoning', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Orchestra Conductor & Courtroom Evidence', 'text': '• Concord: The subject and verb must play in harmony (Singular subject takes singular verb).\n• Critical Reasoning: Distinguishing between a confirmed fact stated in a text versus an unproven assumption made by the author.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '38.1', 'title': 'Subject-Verb Agreement (Concord) Rules', 'content': "• <b>Rule 1: Either/Or & Neither/Nor:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>CLOSER subject</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: 'Neither the manager nor the <i>employees were</i> present.'<br/>• <b>Rule 2: As well as / Together with / Along with:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>FIRST subject</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: 'The <i>captain</i>, along with his crew members, <b>was</b> honored.'<br/>• <b>Rule 3: Each, Every, Everyone, Neither (alone):</b> Strictly <b>SINGULAR</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: 'Each of the students <b>has</b> completed the project.'", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 'A as well as B', verb follows A! In 'Either A or B', verb follows B (the nearest subject)!"}, {'number': '38.2', 'title': 'Common Word Confusions in GATE', 'content': "• <b>Affect vs Effect:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Affect (Verb):</b> To influence. ('The weather affected his mood').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Effect (Noun):</b> The result/consequence. ('The policy had a good effect').<br/>• <b>Complement vs Compliment:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Complement:</b> Completes or enhances something ('Bread and butter complement each other').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Compliment:</b> An expression of praise ('He paid her a nice compliment').<br/>• <b>Principal vs Principle:</b> Principal = head of school / main; Principle = fundamental truth / rule.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Affect is almost always an Action (Verb), Effect is an End-result (Noun). Remember RAVEN: Remember Affect Verb Effect Noun!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Subject-Verb Agreement Selection', 'problem': "Choose the correct verb: 'Neither the professor nor his research students _____ (was / were) able to solve the puzzle.'", 'steps': ["Step 1: Identify conjunction: 'Neither ... nor ...'.", 'Step 2: Rule of Proximity: The verb agrees with the subject closer to it.', "Step 3: The closer subject is 'his research students' (Plural).", "Step 4: Plural subject requires plural verb 'were'."], 'answer': "Correct sentence: 'Neither the professor nor his research students were able to solve the puzzle.'"}], 'exam_traps': ["In reading comprehension, answers that use extreme words like 'always', 'never', 'only' are usually wrong unless explicitly in the text!", 'An assumption is an UNSTATED premise required for the conclusion to hold true.']}}
